\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Canada \highlight\uc2  Morning: C$ Down, Mkt Cautious on  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Fear \b0\par\par\uc2 387 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 August 1998\par 15:44\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TORONTO (Dow Jones)--The Canadian dollar is down against the U.S. dollar in cautious trading early Monday, with the U.S. unit having again moved above the C$1.5200 mark in reaction to Russia's devaluation of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar was trading at C$1.5220 at 9:38 a.m. EDT (1338 GMT), up from C$1.5212 at 8:00 a.m. EDT (1200 GMT) and from C$1.5171 late Friday, which was also the Bank of Canada's official closing level. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the Canadian dollar was pushed lower in an immediate knee-jerk reaction to the news out of Russia. Unlike the German deutschemark however, the Canadian unit failed to stage a comeback as markets digested the news. \par \par \uc2  "I'm kind of surprised that Canada's still so weak," said a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at a major Canadian bank in Toronto. "In Canada, the pressure is now more due to natural demand for U.S. dollars on the back of the September bond maturities, and a bit of speculative interest beginning to seep back into the market." \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar has been expected to come under more pressure from a heavy government bond redemptions calendar over the next eight months, with C$12.8 billion slated to mature in September alone. \par \par \uc2  Some traders also suggested the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent asymetrical \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy in support of the Canadian dollar may be starting to lose some of its sting. \par \par \uc2  "People are beginning to believe that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  can't hold the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  alone, as it looks less and less likely that the bank can raise interest rates," said one trader. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  level was at C$1.5200 on Thursday, and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  watchers said the market is looking for another \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the U.S. dollar begins creeping up towards C$1.5230 Monday. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par These are the exchange rates at 9:38 a.m. EDT (1338 GMT), 8:00 a.m.\par EDT (1200 GMT), and late Friday.\par    U.S./CDN    1.5220      1.5212   1.5171\par    CDN/DEM     1.1787      1.1814   1.1872\par    CDN/YEN     95.99       95.14    96.43\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires 17-08-98\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1344GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1158 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du8h06x59\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
