\par\par \b \uc2  Canadian bonds open little changed, T-bills firm. \b0\par\par\uc2 457 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 August 1998\par 16:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TORONTO, Aug 14 (Reuters) - Canadian bonds opened little changed on Friday, with a lack of significant news keeping activity muted, even as T-bills benefited from the stabilisation of the Canadian dollar. \par \par \uc2  "We've been conditioned to look to international situations for signs of which way things are going. Today, it's a little bit more quiet out there," said Arve Bendiksrud, a fixed-income analyst with TD Securities Inc. \par \par \uc2  Canada's benchmark 30-year bond fell C$0.14 to C$132.16 to yield 5.710 percent. Its U.S. 30-year bond rose 2/32 to yield 5.59 percent. The Canada-U.S. spread rose to 12 basis points from six basis points at Thursday's close. \par \par \uc2  Canada's yield curve underperformed the U.S. curve from the three year area and up. Analysts said Canadian bonds have been under pressure during the past few weeks because of weakness in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  For the first time in years, Canadian bonds are now yielding more than their U.S. counterparts, beginning at the three-year portion of the curve. \par \par \uc2  Hammered by falling commodity prices, the Canadian dollar fell to the lowest point in its 140-year history on August 7, dropping to C$1.5355 ($0.6513). \par \par \uc2  But aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets during the past five sessions benefited the money market on Friday. \par \par \uc2  T-bill prices rose as the Canadian dollar firmed to open at C$1.5178 (US$0.6588), up from C$1.5194 (US$0.6582) at Thursday's close. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to buy Canadian dollars for U.S. dollars around C$1.5200 (US$0.6579). The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has moved from a policy of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to smooth out gyrations in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to aggressively defending the dollar. \par \par \uc2  One money market dealer said the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  the subsequent stabilisation of the dollar has reduced expectations that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will soon have to raise interest rates. \par \par \uc2  "Every morning we used to come in and say 'The bank is going today, the bank is going today', and that tone has changed definitely," the dealer said. \par \par \uc2  "I've not been the biggest fan of the Bank of Canada in the past six months, but they've actually, for now this week, have stemmed the tide," the dealer added. "They've done a good job this week." \par \par \uc2  Canada's three-month when issued T-bill firmed to yield 4.82 percent from 4.86 percent at Thursday's close. \par \par \uc2  ($1=$1.52 Canadian) \par \par \uc2  ((Jeffrey Hodgson, Reuters Toronto newsroom (416) 941-8105, toronto.newsroom@reuters.com)) \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021113du8e03744\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
