\par\par \b \uc2  Toronto stocks seen doing about-face at open. \b0\par\par\uc2 383 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 August 1998\par 14:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab     CHANGE\tab \tab \tab   CHANGE\par TSE300(CLS) 6345.22 -150.13 HI 6510.27\tab    LO 6345.19\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  DJIA(CLS)   8459.50  -93.46 GOLD (LONDON)   US$284.80 +1.40 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par FTSE\tab 5511.60 +112.10 GOLD (COMEX)   US$289.40 +0.10\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par NIKKEI    15123.93 -258.09 LME CASH NICKEL US$4100    +12\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par CANDLR\tab 1.5169\tab  LME CASH ALUM   US$1308.0  +4.0\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par CAN 30-YR   132.24   +0.00 BRENT CRUDE    US$12.05  -0.04\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par --------------------MARKET COMMENT-------------------------\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Toronto stocks were again expected to do an about-face,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par rallying on the back of stronger European markets. * Asian,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Russian markets stabilised in early action.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TORONTO, Aug 14 (Reuters) - Toronto stocks were expected to again do an about-face on Friday, rallying on the back of renewed stability in Asian and Russian markets.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Hong Kong's Hang Seng index finished 8.47 percent higher after the Hong Kong \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the stock and futures markets. Russian stock and debt markets on Friday recovered some of the substantial ground lost in several days of panic-stricken selling.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But Russian analysts remained edgy. There are nagging fears that another financial crisis may be lurking around the next corner.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Toronto's rally could amount to as much as 50 or even 100 points but volatility will persist, said Levesque Beaubien Geoffrion liability trader David Jarvis.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Today is going to be what you call an inside day. We are going to stay between the highs of yesterday and the lows of yesterday," Jarvis said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Toronto will rally."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Toronto Stock Exchange's key 300 Composite Index fell 150.13 points or 2.3 percent to 6345.22 on Thursday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A firmer Canadian dollar and gains in gold should further underpin a positive day for Toronto which has whipsawed from steep losses to sharp gains all week.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was trading around C$1.5163 in early domestic action after a close of C$1.5193. Canada's dollar, nicknamed the loonie for the native bird depicted on the one-dollar coin, dipped to a softer close despite \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on Thursday by the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ((Sarah Edmonds, Reuters Toronto Bureau 416-941-8100 e-mail toronto.newsroom@reuters.com)).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 1998.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021113du8e0361s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
