\par\par \b \uc2  Canadian bonds close higher, T-bills firm. \b0\par\par\uc2 413 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 August 1998\par 23:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TORONTO, Aug 14 (Reuters) - Canadian bonds closed firmer on a buoyant U.S. market on Friday after U.S. economic data came in within expectations. \par \par \uc2  T-bills benefited from a stable Canadian dollar. \par \par \uc2  "The data, surprisingly enough, given the volatility you could have expected around the GM strike, was almost bang on market expectations across the board," said Marcel Kasumovich, assistant economist at Goldman Sachs Canada. \par \par \uc2  U.S. producer prices and industrial production data came in roughly in line with forecasts, suggesting inflation remains negligible in the United States and there is continuing weakness in the manufacturing sector. \par \par \uc2  Canada's benchmark 30-year bond rose C$0.39 to C$132.69 to yield 5.680 percent. Its U.S. 30-year bond rose 24/32 to yield 5.55 percent. The Canada-U.S. spread rose to 13 basis points from six basis points at Thursday's close. \par \par \uc2  "We've seen relative stability in the Canadian dollar, which appears to have kept the Canada-U.S. spreads, particularly in the 10-year sector, roughly unchanged," Kasumovich said. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said Canadian bonds have been under pressure during the past few weeks because of weakness in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . For the first time in a year Canadian bonds are yielding more than their U.S. counterparts, beginning at the three-year portion of the curve. \par \par \uc2  Hammered by falling commodity prices, the Canadian dollar fell to the lowest point in its 140-year history on August 7, dropping to C$1.5355 to the U.S. dollar ($0.6513). \par \par \uc2  But aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets during the past five sessions benefited the money market on Friday. \par \par \uc2  T-bill prices rose as the Canadian dollar firmed to close at C$1.5171 (US$0.6592), up from C$1.5194 (US$0.6582) at Thursday's close. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to buy Canadian dollars for U.S. dollars around C$1.5200 (US$0.6579). The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has moved from a policy of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to smooth out gyrations in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to aggressively defending the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Canada's three-month when issued T-bill firmed to yield 4.84 percent from 4.86 percent at Thursday's close. \par \par \uc2  ($1=$1.52 Canadian) \par \par \uc2  ((Gilbert Le Gras, Toronto Reuters Newsroom, 416 941 8100, fax 416 869 3436, email: toronto.newsroom@reuters.com)) \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021113du8e034nz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
