\par\par \b \uc2  =Canada Bond Redemptions -3: Bond Mkt Seen More Vulnerable \b0\par\par\uc2 368 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 August 1998\par 17:12\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  However, a recent pattern of rapid, often aggressive market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in defense of the Canadian dollar is sending signals that Canada's monetary authorities have seen enough depreciation in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and it's slide has at least been checked, if not reversed. \par \par \uc2  A growing number of analysts now feel that a little more \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stability may be all international investors need to keep at least some of their redemption proceeds in Canada, especially given the fact that Japan and Russia now seem poised to set off a new round of global financial turmoil. \par \par \uc2  "I'm not expecting major turbulence from the redemptions calendar - I think it's been overstated," said Aron Gampel, deputy chief economist at the Bank of Nova Scotia. "Investors have been shunning Canadian securities for some time and what I'm banking on is that international investors will want to at least maintain a core presence in Canada." \par \par \uc2  Most of the potential turbulence over the heavy redemptions period is now seen falling on the Canadian bond market, as an ever-expanding pool of excess liquidity sloshes around over the next eight months trying to find a home. \par \par \uc2  "It's perhaps less of an issue of what's going to happen to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as what's going to happen to spreads between Canada and the U.S.," said Nesbitt Burns' Porter. "I suspect we are going to see a marked deterioration of relative spreads during this heavy rollover period." \par \par \uc2  The case for not rolling over proceeds into other Canadian issues largely hinges on the deterioration in relative yield spreads versus U.S. Treasurys that has occurred over the past few years, most agree. \par \par \uc2  "The original buyers were able to get the maturing bonds at a 60 to 90 basis point yield pickup over U.S. Treasurys," noted one Toronto bond dealer. "Now there's only a small 10-15 basis point pickup in the 5-year and 10-year area. As the stuff matures, are foreigners and other investors going to be as eager to step back in - I doubt it." \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires  08-14-98 \par \par \uc2  1112EDT \par \par \uc2  4218 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020010916du8e01y3m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
