\par\par \b \uc2  WORLD BONDS-Australia preferred over Kiwi, Canada. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Deepthi Wickremasinghe \par \uc2 754 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 August 1998\par 15:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Aug 13 (Reuters) - Investors should consider Australian government bonds over their New Zealand and Canadian counterparts, but all three markets are dogged by \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakness, analysts said on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  turmoil created by the Asian economic crisis has in turn affected government bonds, particularly in Australia and New Zealand but also Canada as well. \par \par \uc2  Added to this New Zealand faces political risk from a possible break up of the country's coalition government, while Australian tax reform proposals have marred the longer term outlook for Aussie bonds. \par \par \uc2  In \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakness prompted the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday, selling U.S. dollars to try to prop up the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The intensification of the Asian crisis has hit these markets especially badly and near term I would be cautious," said Dick Howard, director of economic research at Bank Julius Baer in London. \par \par \uc2  "All three are perceived as commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . We've had weaker commodity prices which has led to weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and that in turn has fed through into higher bond yields." \par \par \uc2  At 1219 GMT, 10-year Australian bonds were yielding 24 basis points over U.S. T-notes. Aussie bonds were trading at 15 basis points through U.S. Treasuries in late May, early June. \par \par \uc2  The New Zealand/U.S. spread was at 96 basis points, having widened from 63 earlier this month. \par \par \uc2  Howard said the at these levels Aussie and Kiwi bonds offered reasonable value. "But near term so long as we continue to get bad news out of Asia the spread could widen further. So investors may yet be able to get better levels," he said. \par \par \uc2  But the outlook for Australian bonds over the next few years is less rosy. Tax reforms unveiled on Thursday have changed the fiscal outlook, reducing the budget surplus for 1999-2000 and 2000-2001. \par \par \uc2  "The market was distinctly soggy in the run up to the tax reform announcement," said Howard. "(Australian Treasurer Peter) Costello didn't announce anything that was any great surprise but even so the idea that the fiscal position going ahead is going to be nothing like as good as the market thought it was has certainly taken the shine off the Australian bond market." \par \par \uc2  James McKay, global market strategist at Commonwealth Bank in London, also preferred Australian debt near term. \par \par \uc2  "With dollar/yen heading down, you would expect that to support the Aussie dollar, which should remain supportive towards the Australian bond market," he said. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen was at 145.60, just off the day's low. \par \par \uc2  McKay said New Zealand debt and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets would suffer from political uncertainty after cabinet ministers from the New Zealand First party walked out on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "While I wouldn't expect that to amount to anything in the end, it is clearly going to have an impact on the market over the coming days," he said. \par \par \uc2  "Nevertheless if the market sells off too much in New Zealand, I would certainly recommend people look at the bonds particularly at the shorter end of the curve." \par \par \uc2  Elisabeth Afseth, fixed income saleswoman at Williams de Broe in London, said political risk had caused the 10-year spread to widen and further underperformance of New Zealand bonds could be expected in coming days. \par \par \uc2  "For the short term I would be quite cautious, but if it goes out to much over 100 it could be a buying opportunity," she said. "On economic fundamentals you are seeing the economy slowing down quite a lot so longer term the bond market should be relatively good." \par \par \uc2  The 10-year Canada/U.S. spread was at 17 basis points compared with minus 35 in mid-April. \par \par \uc2  "Canada is coming back onto the radar screens for investors," said Howard. \par \par \uc2  However, Julius Baer had a zero weighting in Canadian bonds and was not likely to buy any near term, he said. \par \par \uc2  But although still a risky market, Afseth favoured the longer end of the Canadian yield curve. \par \par \uc2  "The spread is unlikely to go negative again but if it stays at roughly current levels in a 10-20 over range for the time being, it offers reasonably good value," she said. \par \par \uc2  "I would either go for safe-haven buying of Treasuries or if you want a bit more risk with it Canada is starting to look quite good." ((Deepthi Wickremasinghe, International Bonds +44 171 542 4041, Fax +44 171 542 5285, uk.governmentbonds.news@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021113du8d0331i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
