\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Canada \highlight\uc2  Afternoon: C$ Flat, Cedes Gains Despite  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 336 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 August 1998\par 22:23\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TORONTO (Dow Jones)--The Canadian dollar ended only slightly higher Wednesday after the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  repeatedly in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, using U.S. dollar sales as a bungee cord to help the Canadian dollar defy the market gravity that keeps pulling it down. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  at three separate occasions on Wednesday. The third round saw the bank reverting to its usual pattern of repeated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at every five-tick of the U.S. dollar against the Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But the repeated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not prevent the Canadian dollar from giving up almost all the gains it made in a strong overnight performance. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar was trading at C$1.5204 at 4:19 p.m. EDT (2019 GMT), from C$1.5125 at 8:00 a.m. EDT (1200 GMT) and from C$1.5220 late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  It dipped as low as C$1.5117 at 8:06 a.m. EDT (1206 GMT) after hints that Japanese monetary authorities could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to defend the yen against the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  Wednesday marks the fifth consecutive day of daily market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and the fourth day it employed its new tactic of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  before its normal trigger levels. \par \par \uc2  But the despite the Bank of Canada's persistent efforts, the Canadian dollar kept up the pattern of decline that first surfaced late in the morning, and then intensified as the U.S. dollar gained ground against most other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Market watchers attributed the U.S. dollar's vigor to developments in Russia, where the Duma decided to delay its consideration of a series of government austerity measures. \par \par \uc2  Rumors of a ruble devaluation also swirled through international financial markets Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "The Russian news here this afternoon is not helping the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The big dollar is gaining strength across the board," noted Bill Mooney, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at the Societe Generale. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 12-08-98 \par \par \uc2  2023GMT \par \par \uc2  1158 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du8c06svi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
