\par\par \b \uc2  Bank moves support Canada dollar, but outlook murky. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Masaru Sato \par \uc2 764 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 August 1998\par 20:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TORONTO, Aug 11 (Reuters) - Continuing Asian turmoil and depressed commodity prices make prospects for the faltering Canadian dollar uncertain, analysts said on Tuesday, despite several recent rounds of Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that have halted what had been a steady decline. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Canada launched a pre-emptive strike on Tuesday morning for the third straight business day, buying Canadian dollars for U.S. dollars in a successful bid to scare off speculators betting on further drops in the value of the domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Following a turnaround prompted by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday, the local dollar had stabilised on Tuesday at around 65.79 U.S. cents, or C$1.5200 to the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar, now in the middle of a five-month downtrend, has recovered from a record low of 65.14 U.S. cents or C$1.5355 to the U.S. dollar set last Friday. However, it is almost six U.S. cents below its level of a year ago of around 72 U.S. cents. \par \par \uc2  "The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been successful so far, at least preventing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from falling further," Sal Guatieri, senior economist at Bank of Montreal, told Reuters Television. "It has diverted speculators' attention to other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  besides the Canadian dollar." \par \par \uc2  But the outlook for the dollar, referred to by Canadians as the "loonie" for the depiction of the loon on the golden one-dollar coin, remains clouded by a number of factors. \par \par \uc2  A recent slip in the U.S. dollar against the Canadian unit is "a pause in a rising trend", and is unlikely to end until the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rises to 67.57 U.S. cents, or C$1.4800 to the U.S. dollar, a level last seen in mid-July, said Martin Autotte, technical analyst at CIBC Wood Gundy Securities. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is being bolstered by flows of capital seeking a safe haven from such troubled regions such as Asia and Russia. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar rose above 147 yen, an eight-year high, in Asian trading on Tuesday on growing concern about the prospects for Japan's bid to rescue its recession-hit economy through banking and tax reform. \par \par \uc2  And Indonesia's government said on Tuesday it had deferred some principal payments on its foreign debt in August, although that this did not involve a default. \par \par \uc2  In addition, confidence in Canadian securities generally could come under scrutiny by international investors if the Canadian stock market continues to plunge and bond prices fall again. Economists see a recent drop in North American stocks as a correction to sharp gains, rather than a free-fall. \par \par \uc2  A wave of selling on Tuesday drove the Toronto Stock Exchange's key index down by more than four percent at one point in the morning before bargain-hunting pared losses. \par \par \uc2  One factor helping stabilise the Canadian dollar was the release last Friday of Canada's July jobs data, which showed higher-than-expected growth and which coincided with Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market estimated to total at least US$500 million. \par \par \uc2  Canada's dollar has also come under pressure from soft commodity prices caused by the Asian crisis, and more recently, from worries over a slower pace of domestic economic growth. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weathered bearish domestic economic data on Tuesday morning and news of a fall in international crude oil prices to a 10-year low. \par \par \uc2  Canada's housing starts fell 6.5 percent in July to a seasonally adjusted annual rate of 123,100 units from a revised 131,700 in June, hit partly by construction strikes in the province of Ontario. \par \par \uc2  "The weaker starts does suggest a further weakness in the Canadian dollar, and by and large the Bank of Canada will tolerate a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  provided it's reflecting weakening fundamentals," Guatieri said. \par \par \uc2  For clues as to the overall strength of the Canadian economy, the market is awaiting the release on August 31 of second quarter gross domestic product (GDP) and current account data. \par \par \uc2  "The GDP report at the end of this month should help refocus the market," said Rob Palombi, senior fixed-income analyst at Standard & Poor's MMS. "In current account we could see a deterioration, but overall people are still looking at the current account deficit to GDP ratio of two percent. That's actually supportive for the Canadian dollar." \par \par \uc2  ((Reuters Toronto newsroom (416) 941-8107, toronto.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021113du8b02xyv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
