\par\par \b \uc2  World  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  -3: Mexican Markets Down Despite Rate Rise \b0\par\par\uc2 239 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 August 1998\par 23:18\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the Mexican peso closed at another period low Tuesday despite the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's efforts to counter the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's volatility by restricting liquidity in the money market, and pushing up interest rates. \par \par \uc2  The peso closed at 9.1920 per dollar, sharply weaker from its Monday close of 9.0190 per dollar. Mexico's stocks also fell 2.7611%. \par \par \uc2  Other markets in the region followed Mexico's lead. Brazil's Bovespa stock index fell some 4%, while the real ended flat against the dollar after the central banks sold dollars. "The tentacles of the weakness of the Japanese yen have fanned out in many directions," said \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Analytic's Gilmore, pointing to the other less obvious victims of the worsening Asian financial crisis. \par \par \uc2  In Canada, the Canadian dollar again finished lower against the U.S. dollar despite another \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Analysts said the move was enough to anchor the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a firm range, but not to promote its recovery. \par \par \uc2  Late Tuesday, the U.S. dollar was trading at C$1.5223 down from C$1.5177 late Monday. \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar has been under considerable pressure from the fallout of the Asian financial crisis as well as the fall in commodity prices and the relative lower yield on Canadian-denominated assets. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 11-08-98 \par \par \uc2  2118GMT \par \par \uc2