\par\par \b \uc2  Canada bonds open weaker, closing U.S. yield gaps. \b0\par\par\uc2 396 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 August 1998\par 16:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TORONTO, Aug 10 (Reuters) - Canadian bonds weaker on Monday in relatively quiet trade, continuing the downward march begun last week. \par \par \uc2  Canada is failing to attract investors because U.S. dollar assets are in vogue at the moment due to global uncertainties and a bearish domestic stock price performance. \par \par \uc2  Most sectors in Canadian bonds are now offering higher yields than those U.S. bonds, reflecting \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  risks. The Canadian dollar stabilised after Friday's massive \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  but its outlook is uncertain. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2 's benchmark 30-year bond (due June 1, 2027) fell C$0.24 at C$133.63 to yield 5.627 percent. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. 30-year bond fell 1/32 to yield 5.63 percent. \par \par \uc2  The U.S.-Canada spread in 30-years was 0.3 basis points from 1.6 points at the previous close here. \par \par \uc2  "The game is over as far as Canadian yields are trading below those in the U.S.," said Jim Webber, director of fixed-income research at TD Securities Inc. "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  can influence certain rates, but it can't direct the whole market." \par \par \uc2  The bond market has been affected by the weakness in the Canadian dollar in recent trade. \par \par \uc2  "The market is still waiting for some kind of signal on what the bank really wants here," he said. "Friday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at least got a lot of people off guard and was very well done. Whether it's enough to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stable beyond the few days, I'm quite doubtful." \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Canada has refrained from raising its key short-term lending rate as a means of boosting the local dollar. Higher borrowing costs for firms and consumers could hurt consumer and business confidence, and it would take more than a rate hike to turn around the current downtrend. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's prime policy is to control inflation, which is still under control. The depreciation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  tends to push up import costs, but it has not had a major impact on the inflation front. \par \par \uc2  The money market was a bit firmer. \par \par \uc2  Canada's three-month when issued T-bill yielded 5.06 percent after 5.10 percent at the previous close here. \par \par \uc2  ((Masaru Sato, Reuters Toronto newsroom (416) 941-8107, toronto.newsroom@reuters.com)) \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021113du8a02t8b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
