\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Canada \highlight\uc2  Afternoon: C$ Boosted Again By BOC  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 325 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 August 1998\par 22:29\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TORONTO (Dow Jones)--The Canadian dollar ended up against the U.S. dollar for the second straight session Monday, once again on the back of an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  to arrest an impending decline. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar was trading at C$1.5175 at 4:25 p.m. EDT (2025 GMT), from C$1.5216 at 8:00 a.m. EDT (1200 GMT) and from C$1.5212 late Friday. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. unit fell as low as C$1.5152 immediately following the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but began inching up again soon afterward, returning to trade in a tight range between C$1.5160 and C$1.5180 for most of the remainder of the session. \par \par \uc2  As with the Friday \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped in to sell U.S. dollars well in advance of its normal 70-tick \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  threshold, striking just as the Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was starting to weaken rapidly towards the C$1.5250 U.S. dollar level. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  watchers agreed the size of Monday's single \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  came nowhere near the size and scope of the previous \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday, when the bank was rumored to have spent at least C$500 million of its monetary reserves to shore up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "It wasn't as aggressive as Friday, but it was far more effective today," said Bill Mooney, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at Societe Generale in Montreal. "Friday the bank was looking to find U.S. dollar buyers, hitting bids all the way down and doing it rather haphazardly. Today, just the perception that the bank was at a certain level led the market to front-run the bank to a certain degree." \par \par \uc2  Dealers described the post-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  afternoon session as tranquil, with light back-and-forth volleys between Canadian dollar buyers and sellers, but no major movements. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 10-08-98 \par \par \uc2  2029GMT \par \par \uc2  1158 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du8a06s9f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
