\par\par \b \uc2  New jobs, massive  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  spur Canadian dollar. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Andrea Hopkins \par \uc2 564 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 August 1998\par 19:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  OTTAWA, Aug 7 (Reuters) - The Canadian dollar rebounded on Friday after massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and news of slightly better than expected job growth in July surprised the market. \par \par \uc2  The ailing dollar, which only yesterday was prompting calls from opposition politicians for the resignation of Finance Minister Paul Martin, gained more than one U.S. cent after the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in mid-morning trade. \par \par \uc2  "(The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) caught bearish Canadian dollar participants with their pants down," said Jeffrey Cheah, a financial market analyst at Standard & Poor's MMS International. \par \par \uc2  "What the bank did this morning is very effective. If anything, it changes the rules. It shows that you can no longer place a risk-free bet against the bank," Cheah told Reuters. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which had been criticised by some for failing to show enough support for the dollar, was "all over" with persistent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The bank's show of support - its second-straight day of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  - halted what had become a freefall by the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The dollar strengthened to 66.20 U.S. cents after the bank spent between $500 million and $2 billion to increase Canada's popularity. The Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lost a half a U.S. cent on Thursday to closed at record low of 65.39 U.S. cents. \par \par \uc2  Cheah said the dollar also got a boost from slightly higher-than-expected Canadian job growth. Although market reaction to the numbers was masked by the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Cheah said the dollar may have improved on the news that 26,600 jobs were created in Canada in July. \par \par \uc2  Canada's unemployment rate remained stagnant at 8.4 percent, as expected, for the fourth straight month as the benefits of the new jobs were offset by a rise in the number of people actively seeking work. \par \par \uc2  Economists surveyed by Reuters had on average expected July employment to rise by only 4,800 after a decline of 35,900 jobs the month before. \par \par \uc2  Some economists downplayed the growth in new jobs because 20,300 of them were only part-time. \par \par \uc2  "(It is) a little bit better than expected, especially when you look at the headline number of 26,600 jobs, but the bulk was in the part-time sector. The full-time jobs were only 6,300, so the headline number was over-exaggerating the employment growth," said Harvinder Kalirai, an economist at I.D.E.A. in New York. \par \par \uc2  Cheah warned that the bounce in the Canadian dollar will be short-lived if Asia's financial crisis continues to worsen, strangling demand for Canadian commodity exports. \par \par \uc2  "Events in Asia could still pose a problem. If the yen continues to weaken significantly it might put pressure on the Chinese government maybe to (devalue) its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ...not the kind of news that I think would be favourable," Cheah said. \par \par \uc2  An attempt by the finance minister to talk up Canada's economic strengths prior to a speech on Thursday evening had little effect. \par \par \uc2  "Very few countries have the productivity record which we have got," Martin said. \par \par \uc2  "No Canadian, certainly no minister of finance, is indifferent in terms of the behaviour of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he told reporters in Orillia, Ontario. \par \par \uc2  ($1=$1.52 Canadian) \par \par \uc2  ((Andrea Hopkins, Reuters Ottawa Newsroom, 613-235-6745, fax 613-235-5890)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021113du8702rpn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
