\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Canada \highlight\uc2  dollar cuts  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  gains, but firm. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,391 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 August 1998\par 18:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par FORECAST RANGE   1.5100/1.5350\tab ASIAN CLOSE    1.5306\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  US$ SUPPORT 1.5040  RESISTANCE 1.5400 HIGH 1.5346 LOW 1.5312 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par CANADA OPEN 1.5313 HIGH 1.5350\tab EUROPE OPEN    1.5310\par PREV CLOSE  1.5294 LOW  1.5105\tab HIGH 1.5334 LOW 1.5307\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ------------------------------------------------------------\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par FRIDAY, AUGUST 7\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TREND: NEUTRAL, ACTIVITY: ACTIVE\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par *\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's dollar cut gains brought on by massive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the Bank of\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , but was still firmer around C$1.5200 (US$0.6579) in late morning trade.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par *U.S. dollar hit day's low of C$1.5105 (US$0.6620), nearly 250 points (2.5\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Canadian cents), from record trading high of C$1.5350 (US$0.6515). *Canada\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par opened weaker at C$1.5313 (US$0.6530), following overnight moves.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par *Higher-than-expected Canada jobs growth in July supportive for CAD.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TORONTO, Aug 7 (Reuters) - The Canadian dollar trimmed earlier large gains that were made on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , but was still firmer at C$1.5230 (US$0.6579) at midday on Friday after opening at C$1.5313 (US$0.6530).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Bank of Canada surprised traders by hitting the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market as early as 0900 EDT/1300 GMT with massive U.S. dollar selling for Canada, continued at various levels and estimated to total US$500 million to US$1 billion. One trader put it between US$1 billion-US$2 billion.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Unlike Thursday's smoothing-out \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  that failed to stop a slide in the Canadian dollar, today's action worked as many traders had been anticipating a possible rate increase at the 0900 EDT/1300 GMT window for money market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , instead of early \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par To maximize the impact, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was waiting "for the best timing" to step in, an authoritative market source said, using a different tactic than it usually does. Normally, the Bank of Canada limits itself to smoothing out fluctuations when the U.S. dollar rises more than 70 points against Canada from the previous day's close.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The first round of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  came around C$1.5350 (US$0.6515), a record trading high for the U.S. dollar and 50-60 points above the previous close here of C$1.5294 (US$0.6539). Persistent \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pushed the U.S. dollar down by nearly 250 points from the high to a day's low of C$1.5105 (US$0.6620).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The source said the bank considers that Canada has not lost investor confidence despite the downtrend for the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and a bearish tone in domestic equity and bond markets. A rebound in Canada's labour market came as a relief to central bankers.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Higher-than-expected growth in Canadian job creation in July, released earlier this morning, supports the limping local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , but overall sentiment is still bearish as traders were jittery over sliding values in Canadian stocks this week and the U.S. dollar was rising above 146 yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "It has become a confidence issue that forced them to do a direct \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," said Mark Chandler, senior economist at Goldman Sachs \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . "It may just be a matter of time now before the returning weakness in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  leads to a rate increase to support that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Conditions changed in the past week to where it has become more of a confidence issue. I still think that it's appropriate for them to hold off, but I also think they may be forced to raise rates," he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "They (central bankers) will see on Monday whether this holds, if it's at these levels, they might hold off on a rate increase. But once again history will tell us that we're not out of the woods on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  yet just based on sterilised \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ."\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's employment rose 26,600 to 14.27 million in July from June. Full-time employment grew by 6,300 from the month before, while part-time employment grew by 20,300.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The jobless rate in July, as expected, was unchanged from June, for the fourth consecutive month, at 8.4 percent.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The underlying trend in full-time jobs is around 20,000-25,000. That's faster than the growth rate in the working-age population and is still consistent with above-trend gross domestic product growth," said Harvinder Kalirai, economist at I.D.E.A. in New York.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Economists on average had expected Canada's July employment to have grown by 4,800 (forecast range: a fall of 20,000 to a gain of 20,000) after a decline of 35,900 in June.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par There was little market reaction to the U.S. July jobs data, which showed non-farm payrolls rose by 66,000, higher than the consensus forecast of 35,000, after a revised rise of 196,000 (prelim: 205,000) in June.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The growth was the weakest since a decline of 48,000 in January 1996. The July data were distorted by the General Motors strike.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The average hourly earnings, closely watched by the market as an indicator for inflation, rose 0.2 percent in July against the consensus forecast of a rise of 0.3 percent and a rise of 0.1 percent in June.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The unemployment rate, as predicted, was unchanged at 4.5 percent.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    In cross trading, the Canadian unit posted a recovery. It rose to 1.1689\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par marks from 1.1567 marks in the early morning and 1.1598 marks at the previous\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par close here. Canada extended gains to 95.97 yen from 95.10 yen at the opening\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par and from 94.40 yen on Thursday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     The Canadian dollar has been on a general downtrend since hitting a high of\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1.3068 marks on April 1, and, in a bigger trend, slipping from a high of 1.3613\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par marks recorded on August 5, 1997.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     The Bank of Canada has refrained from raising its key short-term lending\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par rate to boost the dollar. Higher borrowing costs for firms and consumers could\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par hurt consumer and business confidence, and it would take more than a rate hike\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par to turn around the current downtrend.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's prime policy is to control inflation, which is still\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par under control. The depreciation of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  tends to push up import costs,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par but it has not had a major impact on the inflation front.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Negative factors still exist for the Canadian dollar.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     It is expected to take a long time before the public feels the effect of\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par the anti-recessionary measures taken by Japan's new government and the market\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par speculates that China will be forced to devalue its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to maintain export\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par competitiveness.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     All this uncertainty encourages a capital flow to U.S. dollar assets,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par which are backed by the world's biggest economy and its strongest military.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     "The market hasn't reacted particularly positively to the Japanese package,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par I gather," said Reid Farrill, executive director, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at CIBC Wood\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Gundy Securities. "If North America sells yen, that would be a signal maybe a\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par tough day for Canada, particularly the yen goes through 146 yen."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Looking at the the U.S. dollar topside, the next target is around C$1.5385,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par or psychological level of US$0.6500. Then comes C$1.5400 (US$0.6494).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     The U.S. dollar firmed above 145 yen, then to a two-month high of 146 yen,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par from around 144 yen as the market was disappointed with an inaugural policy\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par speech by Japanese Prime Minister Keizo Obuchi at the start of an extraordinary\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par session of parliament.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Obuchi had nothing new to offer in his bid to tackle the nation's deepening\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par recession as he had already announced earlier plans to cut taxes and reform the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par banking system.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Japan's prime minister traditionally tries to cover so many issues in an\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par inaugural policy speech that the audience gets only few details on each topic.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     In addition, Japan will not come up with detailed tax reform plans, seen as\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par key to boosting confidence, until the year-end, after debates by the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par government's advisory groups. The government is targeting a tax system overhaul\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par effective on April 1, when the next fiscal year starts.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     (Masaru Sato, Reuters Toronto newsroom (416) 941-8107,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par toronto.newsroom@reuters.com))\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Canadian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market stories.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 1998.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021113du8702qrn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
