\par\par \b \uc2  Canada bonds end weaker despite U.S. gains. \b0\par\par\uc2 611 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 August 1998\par 23:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TORONTO, Aug 7 (Reuters) - Canadian bonds ended weaker on Friday, retaining bearish sentiment seen most of this week triggered by the weakness in the Canadian dollar. \par \par \uc2  A continued slide in the domestic stock market has not prompted a fund shift to Canadian bonds. Instead, investors were concerned about the overall weakness in Canadian assets. \par \par \uc2  In contrast, U.S. treasuries ended firmer on the back of the rising U.S. dollar and steady equity prices. \par \par \uc2  The short end of the yield curve pared earlier gains on the back of a jump in the Canadian dollar against the U.S. dollar in early morning trade. \par \par \uc2  Massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  boosted the local dollar, but its outlook is still uncertain. \par \par \uc2  Canada's benchmark 30-year bond (due June 1, 2027) trimmed some earlier losses, quoted down by C$0.18 at C$133.87 to yield 5.614 percent. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. 30-year bond rose 20/32 to yield 5.63 percent. \par \par \uc2  The U.S.-Canada spread in 30-years narrowed further to 1.6 basis points from 6 points at the previous close here. \par \par \uc2  "The primary question for a lot of those investors is the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Mark Chandler, senior economist at Goldman Sachs Canada. "At even yields or with the potential for a stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , I think we can induce some investors into Canadian assets." \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Canada has refrained from raising its key short-term lending rate as a means of boosting the local dollar. Higher borrowing costs for firms and consumers could hurt consumer and business confidence, and it would take more than a rate hike to turn around the current downtrend. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's prime policy is to control inflation, which is still under control. The depreciation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  tends to push up import costs, but it has not had a major impact on the inflation front. \par \par \uc2  Higher-than-expected growth in Canadian job creation in July, released this morning, is supportive for the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has been on a general downtrend since mid-March. \par \par \uc2  Canada's employment rose 26,600 to 14.27 million in July from June. Full-time employment grew by 6,300 from the month before, while part-time employment grew by 20,300. \par \par \uc2  The jobless rate in July, as expected, was unchanged from June, for the fourth consecutive month, at 8.4 percent. \par \par \uc2  Economists on average had expected Canada's July employment to have grown by 4,800 (forecast range: a fall of 20,000 to a gain of 20,000) after a decline of 35,900 in June. \par \par \uc2  There was little market reaction to the U.S. July jobs data, which showed non-farm payrolls rose by 66,000, higher than the consensus forecast of 35,000, after a revised rise of 196,000 (prelim: 205,000) in June. \par \par \uc2  The growth was the weakest since a decline of 48,000 in January 1996. The July data were distorted by the General Motors strike. \par \par \uc2  The average hourly earnings, closely watched by the market as an indicator for inflation, rose 0.2 percent in July against the consensus forecast of a rise of 0.3 percent and a rise of 0.1 percent in June. \par \par \uc2  The unemployment rate, as predicted, was unchanged at 4.5 percent. \par \par \uc2  The money market was little changed after firming on earlier recovery in the Canadian dollar. \par \par \uc2  Canada's three-month when issued T-bill yielded 5.10 percent after 5.11 percent at the previous close here. \par \par \uc2  ((Masaru Sato, Reuters Toronto newsroom (416) 941-8107, \par \par \uc2  toronto.newsroom@reuters.com))< \par \par \uc2    < \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021113du8702qjr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
