\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS- \highlight22\uc2 Canada \highlight\uc2  chooses dollar  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  over rate hike. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Masaru Sato \par \uc2 875 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 August 1998\par 00:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TORONTO, Aug 7 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  defended its battered dollar with massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Friday, staving off a rise in interest rates that could choke off growth in the domestic economy. \par \par \uc2  Economists hailed the move made by the embattled Bank of Canada - early morning selling of U.S. dollars for Canadian that took Canadian dollar bears by surprise. \par \par \uc2  Many traders, who had expected a rise in the bank's key short-term lending rate to defend the dollar, were caught off guard and the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  boosted the Canadian dollar by more than two Canadian cents against the U.S. dollar at one point. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has held off raising rates since it pushed the key rate up by 50 basis points to 5.0 percent in January to fight \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculators, a move that drew criticism at the time. \par \par \uc2  But market concern over a loss of investor confidence in Canadian securities grew this week as stock and bond prices continued to slide and pressure for a rate hike mounted. The Canadian dollar had been ending day after day at record lows. \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar closed stronger on Friday at 65.70 U.S. cents after a record low the previous day of 65.39 U.S. cents. \par \par \uc2  "I'm supportive of the Bank of Canada not raising rates currently," said Andrew Sharpe, executive director at the Centre for the Study of Living Standards in Ottawa. "We do have a larger output gap than in the States, we have higher unemployment. It's not appropriate to raise interest rates right now." \par \par \uc2  Canada's jobless rate has been stuck at 8.4 percent, compared with 4.5 percent in the United States, according to the latest data, for July, released on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Higher borrowing costs for firms and consumers could hurt consumer and business confidence, and it would take more than a rate hike to turn around the current downtrend for the Canadian dollar, several economists said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's prime policy is to keep inflation low. Depreciation of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pushes up import costs, but it has not had a major impact on the inflation front. \par \par \uc2  Sharpe pointed out that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has no incentive to push for lower inflation right now as Canada's consumer price index (CPI) is rising only one percent year on year - the bottom of the bank's 1-3 percent target band for inflation. \par \par \uc2  Economist Kenneth Boessenkool, at C.D. Howe Institute in Toronto, agreed, arguing that a rate hike now could put the economy back into recession later this year. \par \par \uc2  He is actually calling for action in the opposite direction and recently issued a report titled, "Let's Defend the Dollar - With Lower Interest Rates." \par \par \uc2  Slack demand in Asia has driven the Canadian dollar down and caused the weakness in the economy, which has also been hit by strikes at General Motors plants and in the construction sector. \par \par \uc2  The latest data showed that Canada's economy, as measured by the gross domestic product (GDP) declined by 0.2 percent in May after no growth in April. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Canada has reacted to the dollar's fall in the past by raising interest rates, which has produced more weakness in the economy. \par \par \uc2  "You have to get on the right track by reversing those earlier interest rate increases," Boessenkool said in an interview. \par \par \uc2  On suggestions by some economists for income tax cuts as a means of offsetting the impact of what they consider a necessary evil of a defensive rate hike, Boessenkool said: "We have to separate short-term and long-term issues." \par \par \uc2  "For dealing with what's going on in the short term, why the dollar is falling this month, we think that's related to the weakness in the economy. Long-term problems require long-term solutions," he said. \par \par \uc2  Canada has eliminated two thorny structural weaknesses - a huge budget deficit and a high inflation rate - but it still faces a long process in boosting productivity and lowering tax rates to give incentives to spending and investment. \par \par \uc2  "We are not over the hump yet. It's taking us four to five years to fix the deficit problem, it took us six to seven years to get our inflation rate down," Boessenkool said. "It takes 20 years to create those problems, you can't get out in one." \par \par \uc2  Canada has jumped onto the bandwagon of corporate restructuring and social welfare spending cuts, but it may not totally emulate the United States, its biggest trade partner. \par \par \uc2  "As far as social policy goes, we still have a preference for a generous social safety net, and that's why our debt-to-GDP ratio is not to going to decline dramatically over the near term. That's the choice Canadians have made for themselves," said Rob Palombi, senior fixed-income analyst at Standard & Poor's MMS. "It would be difficult for any government to introduce a new round of spending cuts and tax cuts at the same time to bring the debt burden down more quickly." \par \par \uc2  ((Reuters Toronto newsroom (416) 941-8107, toronto.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \p