\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Canada \highlight\uc2  Afternoon: C$ Soars on  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 415 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 August 1998\par 22:03\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TORONTO (Dow Jones)--Backed by massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Canadian dollar enjoyed a dramatic reversal in fortune Friday, registering large gains against its U.S. counterpart in volatile, active trading. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar was trading at C$1.5212 at 4:00 p.m. EDT (2000 GMT), from C$1.5300 at 8:00 a.m. EDT (1200 GMT) and from C$1.5288 late Thursday. The U.S. unit whipsawed through a wide range between C$1.5350 and C$1.5115 in the morning session before settling in a narrower range around the C$1.5200 mark. \par \par \uc2  Market watchers said the size and timing of Friday's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  caught the market off-guard, but most agree it was likely motivated by the Canadian dollar's continued weakness after generally good July jobs data were released in the early morning. \par \par \uc2  As the Canadian unit weakened off towards C$1.5350 early, several waves of U.S. dollar selling by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit the market, in a move that most analysts said was unprecedented in recent years in terms of scope and timing (see story 4238). \par \par \uc2  Following a volatile morning session, traders said the market was largely becalmed in afternoon trading, especially after the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  failed to extend a brief retracement of its morning losses. \par \par \uc2  "There was some thin corporate interest in buying U.S. dollars at lows," said a trader at a major Canadian bank. "That accounted for a bounce of about 100 ticks in the afternoon, but we came off that later." \par \par \uc2  Analysts said it is still too early to tell if Friday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be successful in engineering anything more than a brief turnaround in sentiment for the beleaguered Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but a key development in its favor is likely today's closing level near C$1.5200. \par \par \uc2  "People will likely feel that the bank's efforts were not in vain with any close around C$1.5200 or slightly lower," said Stephen Wateridge, managing director of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the Canadian Imperial Bank of Commerce. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par These are the exchange rates at 4:00 p.m. EDT (2000 GMT), 8:00 a.m.\par EDT (1200 GMT), and late Thursday.\par    U.S./CDN    1.5212      1.5300   1.5288\par    CDN/DEM     1.1701      1.1582   1.1601\par    CDN/YEN     96.11       95.22    94.32\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires 07-08-98\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 2003GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1158 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du8706q6x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
