\par\par \b \uc2  Analyst - Cdn Imperial, Cdn Banks -5:Fund Mgr Buys CIBC Stk \b0\par\par\uc2 377 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 August 1998\par 20:55\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Royal Bank of Canada's (RY) earnings in the fiscal third quarter will be affected to some extent by the industry-wide slowdown in brokerage and underwriting businesses. However, the impact will be significantly less than the impact on Canadian Imperial Bank of Commerce (BCM), said Nabanita Merchant, Royal Bank's head of investor relations. \par \par \uc2  "We have a very different strategy" than Canadian Imperial, Merchant noted. Royal Bank is more focused on its personal and commercial business and wealth-management \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , than on the brokerage business. In addition, Royal Bank hasn't expanded as agressively as Canadian Imperial into servicing U.S. capital markets, Merchant said. \par \par \uc2  A spokesman for Bank of Montreal (BMO) declined to comment on the bank's third-quarter results, except to say the bank has no reason to pre-announce its results. \par \par \uc2  Friday's sell-off in the bank sector comes after a substantial correction in bank stocks in recent weeks. That correction resulted from fears of higher interest rates due to the weak Canadian dollar and concerns over whether planned mergers in the group will get government approval. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Friday by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the Canadian dollar against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  heightened concerns that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might be preparing to raise interest rates Monday. \par \par \uc2  However, analysts figure the rate hike fears aren't a big reason for the sell-off Friday in the bank sector. \par \par \uc2  Veronika Hirsch, president of fund manager Hirsch Asset Management Corp., who bought some Canadian Imperial stock Friday, isn't concerned about an increase in interest rates, because rates would still be relatively low. \par \par \uc2  Hirsch "nibbled" at Canadian Imperial stock, because it's relatively inexpensive. Under the worst-case scenario, Hirsch figures the bank will earn C$3 a share in fiscal 1998, which ends Oct. 31. Based on its recent price of around 37.30 on the Toronto Stock Exchange, the stock is trading at 12 times estimated fiscal 1998 earnings. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Hirsch said the consensus among analysts she uses is for Canadian\par Imperial to earn about C$3.20 a share in fiscal 1998.\par    -By Ben Dummett; 416-943-7807\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) DOW JONES NEWS  08-07-98\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 02:55 PM\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dj00000020010916du87078vm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
