\par\par \b \uc2  Canada Bonds Down, Mkt Watches C$ Rally From The Sidelines \b0\par\par\uc2 366 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 August 1998\par 21:53\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    TORONTO (Dow Jones)--Canadian bonds ended down slightly\par across most of the curve Friday as bond market players seemed\par reluctant to jump on the Canadian dollar bandwagon created by\par the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's massive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par markets.\par    5.00s 2000 99.50   unch     5.33% vs 5.33%\par    5.50s 2002 100.15  dn 0.05  5.46% vs 5.44%\par    6.00s 2008 103.77  dn 0.08  5.50% vs 5.49%\par    8.00s 2027 133.84  dn 0.16  5.62% vs 5.61%\par    10-Yr Spread To U.S. 10-yr: +0.11 vs +0.06\par    CGB SEP 98 123.90 dn 0.07 volume 7,510\par    The Canadian bond market has been undermined in recent\par sessions by the declining Canadian dollar, with bonds putting\par in a particularly bad performance on Thursday, as the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  crashed through new lows and triggered multiple\par market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par    But the Canadian dollar saw a dramatic turnaround Friday\par morning after the Bank of Canada launched a major assault on\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets in defense of the beseiged \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par    In wave after wave of U.S.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  dollar selling, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central\par bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  managed to push the U.S. dollar down from around\par C$1.5350 at 9:18 a.m. EDT (1318 GMT) to C$1.5115 at 9:53 a.m.\par EDT (1353 GMT).\par    The U.S. retraced some ground, and was at C$1.5198 at\par 3:41 p.m. EDT (1941 GMT).\par    But the move may not have been enough to wipe away the\par bond market's worries about the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , suggested Arve\par Bendiskrud, fixed-income analyst at T.D. Securites Inc.\par    "I don't think it's that easy to restore confidence to\par the markets," Bendiskrud said.\par    "Even though things might have worked out today for the\par bank . . . I'm not sure that bond market investors are\par convinced the stronger tone will remain as we get into the\par new week," he said.\par    Trading was active, at least early in the session,\par Bendiskrud said. "We were certainly busy," he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires  08-07-98\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1553EDT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1182 4152 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020010916du8701wwr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
