\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Canada \highlight\uc2  dollar ends at new low despite  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2 1,173 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 August 1998\par 23:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par FORECAST RANGE   1.5220/1.5320\tab ASIAN CLOSE    1.5191\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par US$ SUPPORT 1.5190  RESISTANCE 1.5400 HIGH 1.5220 LOW 1.5182\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par CANADA OPEN 1.5214 HIGH 1.5300\tab EUROPE OPEN    1.5192\par PREV CLOSE  1.5174 LOW  1.5200\tab HIGH 1.5220 LOW 1.5195\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ------------------------------------------------------------\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par THURSDAY, AUGUST 6\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TREND: BEARISH, ACTIVITY: MODERATE\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par *Canada's dollar ended at record low close of C$1.5294 (US$0.6539) after\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par hitting a record trading low of C$1.5300 (US$0.6536). *Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at C$1.5245 (US$0.6560), but traders continued to sell Canada.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par *Canada's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , bonds, stocks all fell today. *U.S. dollar cut gains, but\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par still buoyant above 144 yen. *Focus on Friday release of U.S. and Canada's July\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par jobs data. *Dollar/yen awaiting Japan PM Obuchi policy speech Friday, and\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par development in White House sex and perjury scandal.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  <\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TORONTO, Aug 6 (Reuters) - Hit by a bout of selling, the Canadian dollar ended at another record low close of C$1.5294 (US$0.6539) on Thursday, after hitting a record trading low of C$1.5300 (US$0.6536).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Traders continued to sell \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , ignoring \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which sold U.S. dollars at C$1.5245 (US$0.6560) in morning trade.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Near-term sentiment for Canadian assets is bearish. Stocks, bonds and the local dollar all fell today.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par After opening at $1.5214 (US$0.6573) and briefly touching the U.S. dollar downside of C$1.5195 (US$0.6581), the Canadian dollar was 100 points off its day's high by the end of the day.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Sticking to its normal methods of smoothing out market moves, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  stepped in as the local dollar drifted lower by about 70 points from the previous day's close of C$1.5174 (US$0.6590).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But a lack of communication from Canada's top policymakers on the state of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is drawing criticism from market watchers.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Basically, the credibility issue has been thrown out the window." said David Ebata, senior Canadian market analyst at Technical Data in Boston.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par He is concerned about a loss of investor confidence in the whole of Canada - stocks, bonds, and the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  - following a sell-off in North American stocks on Tuesday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Canada's dollar weakened against several major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In cross trading, the Canadian unit trimmed some losses, but was still weaker at 1.1598 marks from 1.1655 marks at the previous close here, and stayed bearish at 94.40 yen after 94.80 yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Canadian dollar has been on a general downtrend since hitting a temporary high of 1.3068 marks on April 1, and in a bigger trend slipping from a high of 1.3613 marks recorded on August 5, 1997. Against the yen, support for Canada is seen at 93.97 yen and then 93.29 yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Against the U.S dollar, it's a whole new world for chartists and traders.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "I think we're in virgin territory here, so every big figure is a target. I think C$1.5300 (US$0.6536) is my first resistance (for the U.S. dollar)," said Martin Autotte, technical analyst at CIBC Wood Gundy Securities.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "We've already come up 100 points very quickly today, and I think that should put a cap on the Canadian dollar's weakness for the week," he said. "Going into next week, we're definitely targeting C$1.5400 (US$0.6494)."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Although the market was bracing, just in case, for any action by the Bank of Canada in the money market to support the Canadian dollar this morning, a general consensus among economists was that the best policy for the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is to hold off raising interest rates unless the weak \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  starts to undermine economic activity.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The U.S. dollar was buoyant above 144 yen, swinging to the tune of the U.S. stock market performance. Sustained bearishness in Canadian stocks would give downward pressure to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , but a higher finish on the New York stock market today helped the U.S. dollar.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The market is awaiting any news from testimony by Monica Lewinsky, a key figure in the White House sex and perjury scandal, that could deal a blow to the U.S. presidency and suggest a risk for U.S. dollar long positions.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par For clues to the outlook for the yen, traders are watching for any surprise on banking and tax reform from a traditionally lengthy but bland policy speech by Japan's prime minister on Friday at the start of an extraordinary parliamentary session.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "There's been a lot of talk about higher rates, even this morning the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par market has been speculating that they (the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) will do something, but\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par the bank obviously does not want to raise rates and seems prepared to let the\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  go," said Rob Palombi, senior fixed-income analyst at Standard &\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Poor's MMS. "The weakness in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has started to spill over into the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par bond market, but nobody is running for the exits and the selling pressure for\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par bonds has not been that severe."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    As a result, that probably still gives the bank a leeway to stay on hold,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Higher borrowing costs for firms and consumers at the time of global\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par uncertainty (the impact of Asia's financial crisis is being felt in North\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par America) could depress consumer and business confidence further.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     "The only reason the bank would consider raising rates is if it felt the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par large depreciation of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would create inflation risks," Palombi\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     It would be appropriate, he said, for the Bank of Canada to tighten its\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par monetary policy in three to six months' time if the impact of Asia's problems\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par eased and North American economic growth was still firm.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     The market is awaiting the release on Friday of U.S. and Canadian jobs data\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par for July to confirm a slower pace of economic growth in Canada and whether\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par there is any inflationary pressure from the labour market on the U.S. economy.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Economists on average expect Canada's July employment to have grown by\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 4,800 (forecast range: a fall of 20,000 to a gain of 20,000) after a decline of\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 35,900 in June. The General Motors strike in June and July is expected to\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par distort the data. The jobless rate is seen unchanged at 8.4 percent. The weak\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par June report triggered selling of the Canadian dollar.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The U.S. non-farm payrolls are seen rising by 35,000 in July after a rise\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par of 205,000 in June. The unemployment rate is forecast to be unchanged at 4.5\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par percent.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     The average hourly earnings are forecast to be up 0.3 percent in July after\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par a rise of 0.1 percent in June.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (Masaru Sato, Reuters Toronto newsroom (416) 941-8107,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par toronto.newsroom@reuters.com))\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 1998.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021113du8601swf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
