\par\par \b \uc2  Canada dollar hits new lows,  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  steps in. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,075 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 August 1998\par 18:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par FORECAST RANGE   1.5190/1.5300\tab ASIAN CLOSE    1.5191\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par US$ SUPPORT 1.5190  RESISTANCE 1.5400 HIGH 1.5220 LOW 1.5182\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par CANADA OPEN 1.5214 HIGH 1.5295\tab EUROPE OPEN    1.5192\par PREV CLOSE  1.5174 LOW  1.5195\tab HIGH 1.5220 LOW 1.5195\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ------------------------------------------------------------\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par THURSDAY, AUGUST 6\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TREND: BEARISH, ACTIVITY: MODERATE\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Canada's dollar continued to hit new lows in morning trade despite\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to smooth out downward move.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * After moving about 100 points today, it was quoted at $1.5282 (US$0.6544) at midday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Further weak trend set overnight after CAD broke through C$1.5200 late Wednesday in North American time.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Dollar steady above 144 yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Focus on Friday release of U.S. and Canada's July jobs data.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Dollar/yen awaiting Japan PM Obuchi policy speech Friday, and development in White House sex and perjury scandal.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  <\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TORONTO, Aug 6 (Reuters) - The Canadian dollar continued to hit new lows in morning trade on Thursday, quoted much weaker at C$1.5287 (US$0.6542) at midday despite market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to smooth out the downward move.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par After opening at $1.5214 (US$0.6573) and briefly touching the U.S. dollar downside of C$1.5195 (US$0.6581), the Canadian dollar lost 100 points, falling to a a record trading low of C$1.5295 (US$0.6538).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Traders braved the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's move and tested the topside of the U.S. dollar. The bank stepped in as the local dollar drifted lower by about 70 points from the previous day's close of C$1.5174 (US$0.6590).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par And it's a brave new world for chartists.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "I think we are in virgin territory here, so every big figure is a target. I think C$1.5300 (US$0.6536) is my first resistance (for the U.S. dollar)," said Martin Autotte, technical analyst at CIBC Wood Gundy Securities. "I don't think it (the U.S. dollar) is going above C$1.5300 today (on a closing basis), that'll be broken next week."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "We've already come up 100 points very quickly today, and I think that should put a cap on the Canadian dollar's weakness for the week," he said. "Going into next week, we're definitely targeting C$1.5400 (US$0.6494)."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Although the market was bracing, just in case, for any action by the Bank of Canada in the money market to support the Canadian dollar this morning, a general consensus among economists is that the best policy for the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is to hold off raising interest rates unless the weak \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  starts to undermine economic activity.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A further weak tone was set overnight after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  broke through C$1.5200 (US$0.6579) late on Wednesday in North American time, after official trading hours here.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The U.S. dollar stayed above 144 yen, swinging to the tune of the U.S. stock market performance. Sustained bearishness in the equities would put downward pressure on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The market is awaiting any news from testimony by Monica Lewinsky, a key figure in the White House sex and perjury scandal, that could suggest a risk for U.S. dollar long positions.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par For clues to the outlook for the yen, traders are watching for any surprise on banking and tax reform from a traditionally lengthy but bland policy speech by Japan's prime minister on Friday at the start of an extraordinary parliamentary session.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In cross trading, the Canadian unit continued to fall to 1.1571 marks from 1.1655 marks at the previous close here, and slipped to 94.29 yen after 94.80 yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Canadian dollar has been on a general downtrend since hitting a temporary high of 1.3068 marks on April 1, and in a bigger trend slipping from a high of 1.3613 marks recorded on August 5, 1997. Against the yen, support for Canada is seen at 93.97 yen and then 93.29 yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    "There's been a lot of talk about higher rates, even this morning the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par market has been speculating that they (the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) will do something, but\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par the bank obviously does not want to raise rates and seems prepared to let the\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  go," said Rob Palombi, senior fixed-income analyst at Standard &\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Poor's MMS. "The weakness in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has started to spill over into the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par bond market, but nobody is running for the exits and the selling pressure for\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par bonds has not been that severe."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    As a result, that probably still gives the bank a leeway to stay on hold,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Higher borrowing costs for firms and consumers at the time of global\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par uncertainty (the impact of Asia's financial crisis is being felt in North\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par America) could depress consumer and business confidence further.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     "The only reason the bank would consider raising rates is if it felt the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par large depreciation of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would create inflation risks," Palombi\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     It would be appropriate, he said, for the Bank of Canada to tighten its\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par monetary policy in three to six months' time if the impact of Asia's problems\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par eased and North American economic growth was still firm.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     The market is awaiting the release on Friday of U.S. and Canadian jobs data\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par for July to confirm a slower pace of economic growth in Canada and whether\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par there is any inflationary pressure from the labour market on the U.S. economy.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Economists on average expect Canada's July employment to have grown by\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 4,800 (forecast range: a fall of 20,000 to a gain of 20,000) after a decline of\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 35,900 in June. The General Motors strike in June and July is expected to\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par distort the data. The jobless rate is seen unchanged at 8.4 percent. The weak\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par June report triggered selling of the Canadian dollar.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The U.S. non-farm payrolls are seen rising by 35,000 in July after a rise\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par of 205,000 in June. The unemployment rate is forecast to be unchanged at 4.5\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par percent.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     The average hourly earnings are forecast to be up 0.3 percent in July after\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par a rise of 0.1 percent in June.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (Masaru Sato, Reuters Toronto newsroom (416) 941-8107,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par toronto.newsroom@reuters.com))\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 1998.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021113du8601o55\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
