\par\par \b \uc2  Bank Of  \highlight22\uc2 Canada Intervenes \highlight\uc2  -2: Follows Usual Pattern \b0\par\par\uc2 294 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 August 1998\par 17:05\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    TORONTO (Dow Jones)--The Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in\par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market Thursday morning to sell U.S.\par dollars at C$1.5245 in an effort to contain volatility,\par said a trader in Toronto.\par    The U.S. dollar leveled off after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and\par was hovering at C$1.5240 at 10:57 a.m. EDT (1457 GMT).\par    The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's unofficial policy is to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets after intraday moves in either direction\par of 70 ticks or more in order to contain volatility. The\par U.S. dollar closed at C$1.5174 on Wednesday, so Thursday's\par move was consistent with the bank's usual \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par    In cases of sustained pressure against the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ,\par the Bank will \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  repeatedly at each new five-tick\par level the U.S. dollar reaches.\par    \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market sources said they expected to see the\par usual pattern of repeated \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par Thursday morning if the Canadian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  remained under\par sustained pressure.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    The Canadian dollar has been in a relentless slide for\par several weeks, and has set all-time lows in virtually every\par trading session for the last three weeks.\par    Analysts blame the weakness in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on the low\par commodity prices and global uncertainties stemming from the\par economic and financial turmoil in Asia.\par    A recent string of weak economic indicators in Canada\par has added to the pressures against the Canadian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ,\par analysts say.\par    Some market watchers say negative yield spreads between\par Canada and the U.S. are also a key reason for the Canadian\par dollar's weakness.\par    The Bank of Canada has so far resisted pressure to\par defend the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by raising short-term interest rates.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires  08-06-98\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1105EDT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  4346 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020010916du8601vb7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
