\par\par \b \uc2  Toronto stocks swideswiped by more dollar weakness. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Lydia Zajc \par \uc2 524 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Juli 1998\par 23:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TORONTO, July 31 (Reuters) - Any hopes of a sustained recovery for Toronto stocks from recent losses were smashed late on Friday as the key stock index dove 1.6 percent in light volume, sideswiped by fresh weakness in the Canadian dollar. \par \par \uc2  The Toronto Stock Exchange's 300 Composite Index fell 114.53 points or 1.63 percent to close at 6931.43. The index rose on Thursday after dropping for nearly two weeks. Since the index's July 20 opening, at 7441.33, it has lost 6.9 percent. \par \par \uc2  Toronto fared worse on Friday than its U.S. counterpart, which posted a slightly smaller percentage drop, at 1.59 percent, falling 143.66 points to 8883.29. \par \par \uc2  Trading on Canada's largest equities market was thin at 73.8 million shares worth C$1.4 billion as players and investors skipped out ahead of a long weekend. \par \par \uc2  "There's no one around to trade and everybody (who's left) wanted to sell," said Craig Strachan, research services manager at TD Evergreen. \par \par \uc2  Canada's dollar finished the day with a record low close of C$1.5119 (US$0.6614) but was slightly higher than the new low of C$1.5130 (US$0.6609) it hit earlier in the day. \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was forced to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in an attempt to slow the dollar's depreciation after months of losses. \par \par \uc2  As the dollar's value falls, fears increase that the Bank of Canada will be forced to raise short-term interest rates to spur investor interest in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "We're off another quarter cent, and that's whacking our interest-sensitive stocks," Strachan said. Interest-sensitives such as banks generally see their profits shaved once rates rise. \par \par \uc2  Nearly a quarter of Toronto's 300 index is composed of financial services stocks. The sector fell 1.89 percent. \par \par \uc2  Toronto got no help from the U.S., Strachan added, where sentiment was bearish over lower-than-expected corporate earnings results. \par \par \uc2  Strachan was pessimistic about the outlook for the Canadian market. "We're not going to see a bottoming in our market until we see a bottoming in our dollar." \par \par \uc2  All of the TSE's 14 sub-indexes fell, led by golds, off 3.89 percent, consumer products, down 3.32 percent and retail, off 2.05 percent. \par \par \uc2  Declines beat out advances 574 to 391 while 309 stocks closed flat. \par \par \uc2  Markets will be closed on Monday in most of Canada, with the exception of Quebec, for the August civic holiday. \par \par \uc2  TransAlta Corp.  was among the most active stocks, falling C$0.40 to C$21.50. Its New Zealand division is at a critical juncture: within three months it must decide which branch to sell to become either a lines business or an energy company in order to comply with new legislation. \par \par \uc2  Linamar Corp.  fell nearly 10 percent, or C$2.65 to C$25.10, because of disappointing earnings of C$0.33 a share in the second quarter versus a year-ago C$0.36. \par \par \uc2  ($1 = $1.51 Canadian). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du7v00yna\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
