\par\par \b \uc2  Toronto stocks dive in thin volume, hurt by dollar. \b0\par\par\uc2 636 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Juli 1998\par 23:33\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab     CHANGE\tab \tab \tab   CHANGE\par TSE300     6931.43 -114.53 HI 7069.14\tab    LO 6931.43\par DJIA\tab 8883.29 -143.66 GOLD (LONDON)   US$288.85 -1.15\par FTSE\tab 5837.00  -73.70 GOLD (COMEX)   US$290.50 -2.70\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  NIKKEI    16378.97 +177.37 LME CASH NICKEL US$4325   -109 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par CANDLR\tab 1.5119\tab  LME CASH ALUM   US$1324.0 -22.3\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par CAN 30-YR   134.52   +0.12 BRENT CRUDE    US$13.10  +0.02\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par --------------------MARKET COMMENT-------------------------\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Toronto stocks posted a sharp drop in late, thin trading on\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Friday, as concerns about the tumbling Canadian dollar spiked.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Trading skimpy at 74 million shares worth C$1.4 billion.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TORONTO, July 31 (Reuters) - Any hopes of a sustained recovery from recent losses were smashed late on Friday as Toronto's key stock index dove 1.6 percent in light volume, sideswiped by fresh weakness in the Canadian dollar.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Toronto Stock Exchange's 300 Composite Index fell 114.53 points or 1.63 percent to close at 6931.43. The index rose on Thursday after dropping for nearly two weeks. Since its July 20 opening, at 7441.33, the index has lost 6.9 percent.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Toronto on Friday fared worse than its U.S. counterpart, which posted a slightly smaller percentage drop, at 1.59 percent, falling 143.66 points to 8883.29.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Trading on Canada's largest equities market was thin at 73.8 million shares worth C$1.4 billion as players and investors skipped out ahead of a long weekend.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "There's no one around to trade and everybody (who's left) wanted to sell," said Craig Strachan, research services manager at TD Evergreen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Canada's dollar finished the day with a record low close of C$1.5119 (US$0.6614) but slightly higher than the new low of C$1.5130 (US$0.6609) it hit earlier in the day. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was forced to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in an attempt to slow the dollar's depreciation after months of losses.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par As the dollar's value falls, fears increase that the Bank of Canada will be coerced into raising short-term interest rates to spur investor interest in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "We're off another quarter cent, and that's whacking our interest-sensitive stocks," Strachan said. Interest-sensitives such as banks generally see their profits shaved once rates rise.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Nearly a quarter of Toronto's 300 index is composed of financial services stocks. The sector fell 1.89 percent.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Toronto got no help from th U.S., Strachan added, where sentiment was bearish over lower-than-expected corporate earnings results.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Strachan was pessimistic about the outlook for the Canadian market. "We're not going to see a bottoming in our market until we see a bottoming in our dollar."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par All of the TSE's 14 sub-indexes fell, led by golds, off 3.89 percent, consumer products, down 3.32 percent and retail, off 2.05 percent.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Declines beat out advances 574 to 391 while 309 stocks closed flat.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Markets will be closed on Monday in most of Canada, with the exception of Quebec, for the August civic holiday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TransAlta Corp.  was among the most active stocks, falling C$0.40 to C$21.50. Its New Zealand division is at a critical juncture: within three months it must decide which branch to sell to become either a lines business or an energy company in order to comply with new legislation.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Linamar Corp.  fell nearly 10 percent, or C$2.65 to C$25.10, because of disappointing earnings of C$0.33 a share in the second quarter versus a year-ago C$0.36.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ($1 = $1.51 Canadian)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ((Lydia Zajc, Reuters Toronto Bureau (416) 941-8109)).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 1998.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du7v00xna\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
