\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar closes at new low,  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  buys. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,535 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Juli 1998\par 23:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par FORECAST RANGE    1.5030/1.5160\tab  ASIAN CLOSE     1.5031\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  US$ SUPPORT 1.4970  RESISTANCE 1.5270 HIGH 1.5040 LOW 1.5015 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par CANADA OPEN 1.5035 HIGH 1.5130\tab   EUROPE OPEN     1.5022\par PREV CLOSE  1.5063 LOW  1.5035\tab   HIGH 1.5032 LOW 1.5022\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ------------------------------------------------------------\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  FRIDAY, JULY 31\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  TREND: BEARISH, ACTIVITY: MODERATE\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par *Canada's dollar ended at a record low close of C$1.5119 (US$0.6614), but off a record trading low of C$1.5130 (US$0.6609) hit in the early afternoon, at which level Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to slow pace of depreciation. *Sentiment turned bearish after news Canada's May GDP fell and U.S. Q2 GDP came in stronger than forecast. *U.S. dollar staying firm above 144 yen. *Latest U.S. dollar rise sparked by Thursday's comment by Japan's new finance minister that fate of yen and stocks should be left to markets. *Canada's markets closed, except for Quebec, on Monday for holiday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TORONTO, July 31 (Reuters) - The Canadian dollar wrapped up an eventful week sharply lower against the U.S. dollar, ending at a record low close of C$1.5119 (US$0.6614), more than 100 points weaker than a week ago.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Monica Lewinsky gave an unintentional boost to the loonie on Tuesday, when she secured immunity from prosecution in the White House sex scandal. But on Thursday Japan's new finance minister, Kiichi Miyazawa, hit the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  indirectly with comments that the market took as a hands-off policy towards the falling value of the yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The latest trigger pushing down the Canadian dollar was the announcement on Friday morning of the weakest economic growth data in two years. Before the Bank of Canada stepped into the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market to cap the slide, the Canadian dollar hit a record trading low of C$1.5130 (US$0.6609) in the afternoon.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par That's the level at which the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bought Canadian dollars for U.S. dollars. Stop-loss selling beat down the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from C$1.5035 (US$0.6651) at the opening and C$1.5063 (US$0.6639) at Thursday's close.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has a policy where they \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  approximately 70 to 75 points from the previous day's close. And we hit that level, so they \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . It's regular business as usual for the Bank of Canada," Steven Butler, director of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at Scotia Capital Markets, told Reuters Television.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Weaker-than-forecast May economic data for Canada, released this morning, ended a brief pause in Canada selling, prompting the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to breach the previous record low of C$1.5094 (US$0.6625) set here on Thursday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Once we got that data out at 8:30, it really became a full-blown conclusion that Canada would break C$1.5100 (US$0.6623)," said Reid Farrill, executive director, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at CIBC Wood Gundy Securities.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Until late morning, the Canadian dollar resisted one-way selling after slipping immediately after the 0830 EDT/1230 GMT release of two sets of key North American data.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Canada's gross domestic product (GDP), hit by widespread weakness in the economy, fell by 0.2 percent in May after being unchanged in April. The consensus forecast pointed to mild growth of 0.1 percent.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par It marked the first time in two years the economy has failed to grow in two consecutive months.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Statistics Canada foresees GDP growth of around two percent on an annual basis in the second quarter, pulled down by strikes at General Motors' factories and in the construction sector, after 3.7 percent in the first quarter.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In contrast, the U.S. second-quarter GDP rose 1.4 percent from upwardly revised growth of 5.5 percent in the first quarter. The Q2 growth was the weakest since a rise of 0.4 percent in Q2 1995. The average forecast called for a rise of 0.2 percent in the April-June quarter.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In cross trading, the Canadian unit shed earlier gains, falling to 1.1742 marks from 1.1816 marks at the previous close here. Canada slipped a bit but was still quoted higher at 95.58 yen from 93.37 yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Canada's financial markets will be closed, except for Quebec, on Monday for a holiday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The next key data, July employment figures for both U.S. and Canada, come up on Friday, August 7.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Economists on average expect Canada's July employment to have grown by 4,800 (forecast range: a fall of 20,000 to a gain of 20,000) after a decline of 35,900 in June. The jobless rate is seen unchanged at 8.4 percent. The weak June report triggered selling of the Canadian dollar.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The U.S. non-farm payrolls are seen rising by 35,000 in July after a rise of 205,000 in June. The unemployment rate is forecast to be unchanged at 4.5 percent.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Meanwhile, Canada's overall monetary condition, measured by the monetary conditions index (MCI) published and closely watched by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , loosened further this week, along with the depreciation of the Canadian dollar. Traders expect the Bank of Canada to hold off raising its key lending rate unless the weak \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  induces sharply higher import prices and pushes up inflation, or speculative selling of Canada's dollar causes a loss of investor confidence.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Butler said it was hard to pinpoint a specific point on the MCI scale that could prompt the bank to raise interest rates.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "At this point they seem very content to sit back and let the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  run its course," he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Bank of Canada's weekly calculation of the MCI hit a record low this week, according to figures released by the bank on Friday. The bank gave a figure of -6.32 for Wednesday, going beyond the previous record low of -6.26 on June 25, 1997. The more negative the figure, the more relaxed monetary conditions are deemed to be.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The index has slipped further since Wednesday, according to Reuters constantly updated calculations, hitting -6.405 at 1330 EDT (1730 GMT) on Thursday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has said it would expect a wider fluctuation in monetary conditions, measured through movements in short-term interest rates and the Canadian dollar's value.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "It (the weak GDP) is definitely negative for the Canadian dollar. It kind of confirms the trend we've seen in other monthly indicators... manufacturing shipments and new orders are down, exports have been down, a sign of weakness in some aspects of consumer spending," Daniel Kelly, director of financial markets at CIBC Wood Gundy, told Reuters Television.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "This just confirms that the Bank of Canada is between a rock and a hard place. They've got very low inflation in Canada, below the target, but weakening growth, so there's not much of rationale for them to raise interest rates and try to strengthen the Canadian dollar," he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par He said Canada's consumer confidence appeared to have deteriorated recently along with the 20-week decline in the value of the Canadian dollar, but pointed out that the weaker \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which makes Canadian exports price-competitive, has worked as a buffer against soft commodity prides, boosting profit margins for the natural recourse sector and preventing it from laying off workers.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The U.S. dollar stayed firm above 144 yen on fading fears of Bank of Japan \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to stem the yen's slide.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The U.S. unit started its latest upward move on Thursday, when Canada's dollar fell prey indirectly to a comment by Japan's new finance minister, Kiichi Miyazawa, that the fate of the yen and stocks should be left to the markets. The U.S. dollar jumped on his comment as traders took it to mean that no \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was imminent to boost the yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Overnight, a senior Finance Ministry official in Tokyo quoted Miyazawa as saying that Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy was unchanged.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On the domestic front, traders generally hailed the approach to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market taken by Canadian Finance Minister Paul Martin, who stressed the underlying strength of the nation's economy and declined comment on how the government would rescue the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Martin, in response to questions, pointed to Canada's improved economic fundamentals, telling reporters in Ottawa on Tuesday that Canada's productivity had increased, the federal budget was estimated to be balanced this year, and inflation was low.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Martin said he was not "indifferent" to the Canadian dollar's fall.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But his boss, Prime Minister Jean Chretien, did not have much luck in dealing with the market on the following day.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par He stuck to the same line as Martin's, telling reporters that Canada's economy was strong with low inflation. But the timing was bad: the Canadian dollar had already been under downward pressure from stop-loss orders and slumped as he made that comment.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Chretien was in Saskatoon, Saskatchewan, ahead of the annual premiers' meeting there next week.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (Masaru Sato, Reuters Toronto newsroom (416) 941-8107, toronto.newsroom@reuters.com))\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 1998.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du7v00xkb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
