\par\par \b \uc2  Canada bonds end mixed on U.S./Canada GDP data. \b0\par\par\uc2 878 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Juli 1998\par 19:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TORONTO, July 31 (Reuters) - Canadian bonds ended a shortened trading day mixed on Friday, with the long end of the yield curve slightly firmer on the latest economic data and the short end hit by a sharp drop in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Trading was relatively quiet as the bond market closed early here at 1300 EDT/1700 GMT on Friday ahead of a long weekend. Financial markets will be closed on Monday in Canada except for Quebec. \par \par \uc2  Canada's benchmark 30-year bond (due June 1, 2027) rose C$0.23 to C$134.63 to yield 5.572 percent. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. 30-year bond fell 2/32 to yield 5.73 percent. The U.S.-Canada spread was 16 basis points after 14 points at the previous close here. \par \par \uc2  Long-term bond holders were relieved by the U.S. second quarter gross domestic product (GDP) that came in stronger than some forecasts but showed price stability. That suggests that the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will hold off raising interest rates. \par \par \uc2  Canada's long bonds firmed also because the weak May GDP data for Canada prompted expectations that the market will be free of inflationary pressure for the time being. \par \par \uc2  U.S. treasuries suffered from selling as some players were disappointed that steady growth in the U.S. economy, albeit at a slower pace, means there is no need for the Federal Reserve to lower its key lending rate. Some economists had forecast a decline the second quarter. \par \par \uc2  Chicago Purchasing Managers July indices, which showed robust economic activity, also weighed on the U.S. bond market. But the price index showed a falling trend since April. \par \par \uc2  U.S. second-quarter gross domestic product (GDP) grew 1.4 percent after upwardly revised growth of 5.5 percent in the first quarter. The Q2 growth was the weakest since a rise of 0.4 percent in Q2 1995. The average forecast called for a rise of 0.2 percent in the April-June quarter. \par \par \uc2  In contrast, Canada's GDP, hit by widespread weakness in the economy, fell by 0.2 percent in May after being unchanged in April. The consensus forecast pointed to marginal growth of 0.1 percent. \par \par \uc2  Statistics Canada foresees GDP growth of around two percent on an annual basis in the second quarter, depressed by strikes at General Motors' factories and in the construction sector, after 3.7 percent in the first quarter. \par \par \uc2  "The fact that the deflator eased in the U.S. in the second quarter offers more evidence that inflation is not picking up and therefore that we're not going to see a hike by the Fed in the near term, and I think that's helping the long end of the Canadian market outperform the U.S. right now," said Rob Palombi, senior fixed-income analyst at Standard & Poor's MMS. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. GDP price index rose 0.8 percent in the second quarter, slower than a downwardly revised rise of 0.9 percent in the previous quarter. \par \par \uc2  The U.S GDP deflator rose 0.9 percent in the April-June quarter after a rise of a downwardly revised rise of 0.8 percent in the January-March quarter. \par \par \uc2  The front end of the Canadian yield curve was weaker as the local dollar, after posting mild gains overseas overnight, plunged through key support levels and hit new lows before the Bnak of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  at C$1.5030 (US$0.6609). \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Canada's weekly calculation of its monetary conditions index (MCI) hit a record low this week, according to figures released by the bank on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The bank gave a figure of -6.32 for Wedneday, going beyond the previous record low of -6.26 on June 25, 1997. The more negative the figure, the more relaxed monetary conditions are deemed to be. \par \par \uc2  The index has slipped further since Wednesday, according to Reuters constantly updated calculations, hitting -6.405 at 1330 EDT (1730 GMT) on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has said it would expect a wider fluctuation in monetary conditions, measured through movements in short-term interest rates and the Canadian dollar's value. \par \par \uc2  The money market was quiet, fluctuating in a narrow range along with the move in the Canadian dollar. \par \par \uc2  "(It was) a little bit better bid this morning when the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was firmer, but gave up that ground as the (Canadian) dollar started to sell off again," said Walter Posiewko, money market trader at Royal Bank Investment Management Inc. "We're totally in sympathy with the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  right now." \par \par \uc2  Canada's three-month when issued T-bill yielded 5.04 percent after 5.02 percent at the previous close here. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Canada on Friday set special purchase/resale agreements at 5.0 percent to take pressure off tight conditions in the overnight rate movement at the end of the quarter for the bank. It is a technical move by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the call rate within its target range of 4.5-5.0 percent, and has no policy implications. \par \par \uc2  ((Masaru Sato, Reuters Toronto newsroom (416) 941-8107, toronto.newsroom@reuters.com)) \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021113du7v035hf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
