\par\par \b \uc2  Chastened Chretien now mum on flagging Canadian dollar. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Jeffrey Jones \par \uc2 479 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Juli 1998\par 22:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SASKATOON, Saskatchewan, July 29 (Reuters) - Prime Minister Jean Chretien sidestepped questions over the flagging Canadian dollar on Wednesday in an apparent effort to avoid a past gaffe that served only to drive the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  down further. \par \par \uc2  Chretien, in the prairie province of Saskatchewan to meet with Premier Roy Romanow before a meeting of provincial leaders next week, declined to comment to reporters on the dollar other than to point out that the economy was strong and inflation low. \par \par \uc2  "The economy is performing very well at this time. It is our role to make the economy perform very well," he said, noting that annual inflation was less than one percent last month. \par \par \uc2  Chretien's Liberal party government has come under increasing fire from economists and opposition politicians over what they view as a lack of action on the ailing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has recently plumbed record low after record low against the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  Many analysts and observers have said he should talk up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  - to leave the possibility in speculators' minds that they might get burned. \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar, often called the "Loonie" because of the aquatic bird's image on the coin, slipped in value again on Wednesday. It ended at a record low close of C$1.5063 (US$0.6639) on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Chretien's last public \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , on July 14, was to tell reporters after a cabinet meeting in Ottawa that the weaker dollar helped Canadian exports and tourist spots. \par \par \uc2  Asked then if he was happy with the weak dollar he said: "It's not a question of being happy. It's a reality of life." \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the nonchalance displayed by the comments led traders to drive the dollar's value down further. \par \par \uc2  During Wednesday's brief press conference behind a Saskatoon hotel, Chretien also avoided commenting on the possibility of instituting tax cuts as a way of supporting the dollar, instead inviting reporters to ask him the question again before the next fiscal budget, scheduled for February. \par \par \uc2  There have been suggestions from economists and politicians that the best way to boost demand for the Canadian dollar would be to cut taxes. Others have countered that this could raise concerns among international investors about the government's commitment to debt and deficit reduction. \par \par \uc2  Despite vague market expectations of some action, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has refrained from \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and has held off raising key lending rates as a means of attracting investors to Canadian assets. \par \par \uc2  Much of Canada's weakness derives from soft commodities prices as the nation still relies on exports of oil, gas, and farm and forest products for a substantial part of national income. \par \par \uc2  In the eyes of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders, there are few fundamental factors supporting Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the moment. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010924du7t01byb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
