\par\par \b \uc2  Canada dlr crosses key C$1.50, heads to new lows. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Paul Casciato \par \uc2 793 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Juli 1998\par 13:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, July 27 (Reuters) - The Canadian dollar fell past a key psychological barrier at C$1.50 ($0.6666) to set new record lows against the U.S. dollar on Monday, undermined by interest rates below those in the U.S and a fragile economy. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the Canadian dollar set a new historic low of C$1.5022 ($0.6656) after grinding down on hawkish interest rate comments by U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan at his Humphrey-Hawkins testimony last week. \par \par \uc2  "That helped it and there are a lot of economists jumping on board saying the Canadian dollar can achieve really low targets," said John Glover, vice president and director of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the Toronto-Dominion Bank in London. "Our own economist came out over the weekend and said 50 cents (U.S.) was not out of the question." \par \par \uc2  Last week Greenspan warned U.S. lawmakers that the risk of inflationary pressures continued in the U.S. economy and that further strength in wage inflation could force the Federal Reserve to raise U.S. interest rates. \par \par \uc2  Such a rise would widen the interest rate differential between higher U.S. rates and lower Canadian ones. \par \par \uc2  The fall of Canadian interest rates to levels below those of the U.S in the last two years has steadily ground down the Canadian dollar. \par \par \uc2  On Monday U.S. Federal Reserve funds were at 5.50 percent, while the Bank of Canada's operating band for overnight funds was at 4.5 to 5.0 percent. \par \par \uc2  Affectionately known by Canadians as the "loonie" after the waterfowl depicted on the country's one dollar coin, the Canadian dollar has lost more than seven U.S. cents over the last two years and 22 U.S. cents in the last six years. \par \par \uc2  "They've got interest rates below the U.S. and while that happens why buy Canada," said one dealer at a major Canadian bank in London. "You might as well invest in the U.S.." \par \par \uc2  At 0945 GMT the Canadian dollar was trading at 1.5000/10 compared with 1.4984/89 in late European trading on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the Canadian dollar has also been left exposed by a hamstrung Bank of Canada, which cannot raise interest rates to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for fear of squelching a fragile economy. \par \par \uc2  They said they had expected the Bank of Canada to appear in defence of the Canadian dollar ahead of 1.50 on Friday and its failure to appear prompted more selling. \par \par \uc2  "We thought on Friday, the Bank of Canada would have been in early to defend this 1.50 level and there was absolutely no sign of that," said a trader. \par \par \uc2  They said the market would focus on the hours when the Bank of Canada begins its trading \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  around 1355 GMT on Monday for a possible rate rise to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "We will see what happens at the window," another trader said. "I don't think anyone will be long dollars ahead of that." \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy Canadian dollars for U.S. dollars on July 15 at the C$1.4870 ($0.0.6725) level. \par \par \uc2  "I spoke to them (the Bank of Canada) last week and they are not happy about where the dollar is," said Ian Amstad, economist at Banker's Trust in London. \par \par \uc2  But he said low inflation, weak commodity prices and a strike by General Motors Corp workers, made the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  unwilling to raise the cost of financing. "With inflation running at 0.8 percent on their underlying measure and some of their real economy numbers looking a little bit soft they are reluctant to move," Amstad said. \par \par \uc2  He said the Bank of Canada is focused on its Monetary Conditions Index (MCI), as a measure of monetary policy effects and an indicator when an adjustment could be needed. \par \par \uc2  On Friday, the Bank of Canada reported the MCI fell to its lowest level in more than a year to -6.16. \par \par \uc2  The index measures the the three-month prime corporate paper rate and the Canadian dollar index against the 10 major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The index base is January 1987. The more negative the index, the looser monetary conditions are deemed to be. \par \par \uc2  "Now it's a matter of the market deciding when the MCI is at a level where the Bank (of Canada) has got to do something and we're already through the levels where everyone had thought that would be the case now," Glover said. \par \par \uc2  ((Paul Casciato, London newsroom +44 171 542 8968 fax +44 171 542 5293, paul.casciato@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du7r0045t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
