\par\par \b \uc2  Canada dollar ends at new low, near C$1.50. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,179 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Juli 1998\par 23:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par FORECAST RANGE   1.4940/1.5010\tab ASIAN CLOSE    1.4964\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par US$ SUPPORT 1.4890  RESISTANCE 1.5035 HIGH 1.4970 LOW 1.4945\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par CANADA OPEN 1.4953 HIGH 1.4999\tab EUROPE OPEN    1.4962\par PREV CLOSE  1.4956 LOW  1.4948\tab HIGH 1.4955 LOW 1.4944\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ------------------------------------------------------------\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par FRIDAY, JULY 24\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TREND: BEARISH, ACTIVITY: MODERATE\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par *Canada's dollar ended at a record low close of C$1.4991 (US$0.6671), just off\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par key level of C$1.5000 (US$0.6667). *The Bank of Canada's record shows CAD\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par touched record trading low of C$1.4999 (US$0.6667) in a nervous afternoon\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par session. *Market consolidating before breaching $1.5000. *Canada also on\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par downtrend against mark, yen. *Canada's May GDP, due out Friday, forecast weak\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par at +0.1 (range: flat to +0.2) after being flat in April. *U.S. Q2 GDP, also due\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Friday, seen down, growing only 0.2 pct after 5.4 percent growth in Q1.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TORONTO, July 24 (Reuters) - The Canadian dollar ended the week at yet another record closing low, C$1.4991 (US$0.6671), just off the key psychological point of C$1.5000 (US$0.6667).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In a nervous afternoon session, the Canadian unit slipped to a record trading low of C$1.4999 (US$0.6667), according to the Bank of Canada.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The U.S. dollar was marking time before testing the next key resistance, $1.5000 (US$0.6667), as oscillator charts show the U.S. dollar was slightly overbought. Traders have become more cautious about challenging that level, fearing that the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would step in with decisive market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to sell U.S. dollars.\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is on a downtrend against the mark, and generally against the yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In cross trading, the Canadian unit continued to slip to 1.1872 marks from 1.1926 marks at the previous close here, the lowest level since mid-January 1997, when Canada's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was recovering fast from a bottom. Canada trimmed losses to be quoted at 94.41 yen, little changed from 94.35 yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    The Canadian unit has been sliding against the U.S. dollar continuously for\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par the past 19 weeks, starting at the C$1.4070/80 level (US$0.7107/0.71020).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     A recent spate of weak domestic economic data have weighed on the battered\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Canadian dollar. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has also been under downward pressure from soft\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par commodities prices, which were brought on by slack Asian demand. Capital has\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par been flowing into U.S. dollar assets as they maintain their safe-haven status.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Recovery in commodities prices are key to a pickup in the falling local\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par dollar as Canada is still dependent on exporting natural resources.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Canada's May gross domestic product (GDP), due out on Friday, is forecast\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par to show weak growth at 0.1 percent (range: flat to 0.2 percent) after being\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par flat in April.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     The U.S. GDP for the second quarter, also due Friday, is seen down, growing\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par only 0.2 percent after 5.4 percent growth in the first quarter.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     In morning trade on Friday, traders sold local dollars after seeing no\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par action to boost the Canadian dollar by Canada's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in its daily money\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was stable afterwards, with traders squaring their positions\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ahead of the weekend, until rounds of selling emerged in late afternoon trade.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Speculation over a key lending rate increase by the bank comes and goes.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Bank of Canada has resisted occasional market demand for a higher interest\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par rate as a means of attracting investors with higher returns on Canadian dollar\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par assets.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The time for window (for money market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) past, it came and went,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par they didn't hike as I think a lot of Canadians had expected, and Americans were\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par looking for a bit of a rate hike today for some reason," one trader said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Stop-loss selling of U.S. dollars were seen at C$1.4930/35\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (US$0.6698/0.6696), a support level for the U.S. dollar.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     "It's natural that people are trying to square up or get out if we break\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par that," the trader said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Uncertainty also persisted as some option-related moves are anticipated as\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par players hedge their positions by exercising or not exercising their rights to\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par buy or sell dollars at certain levels in the future.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     "I think there are some option (contracts), it will be only natural that\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par some option triggers around the C$1.5000 level, whether or not some guys would\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par be selling at that level or some guys trying to buy remains to be seen," the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par trader said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par While the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is biased towards tighter monetary policy to\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par pre-empt any inflationary pressure, the Bank of Canada is trying to balance\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par differing economic conditions in the country.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Ontario, Canada's industrial and financial centre, is leading other\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par provinces with healthy consumer spending, but British Colombia is still\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par depressed by slack demand for commodities and wood products in Asia.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In key congressional testimony this week, U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Alan Greenspan said higher U.S. inflation would outweigh the downside risk\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par arising from the Asia's economic downturn.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "While we expect that the situation will develop relatively smoothly, the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par committee believes that, given the current tightness in labour markets, the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par potential for accelerating inflation is probably greater than the risk of\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par protracted, excessive weakness in the economy," he said on Tuesday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On Friday Japan's ruling Liberal Democrats elected bland Foreign Minister\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Keizo Obuchi as the new party leader. He will succeed Prime Minister Ryutaro\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Hashimoto after clearing parliamentary votes expected late July or early\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par August.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     His election came as no surprise as it had already been priced into\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par markets.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     For the coming weeks and months, the downside risk for the yen and Japanese\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par equities is any disappointment over the pace of banking and tax system reform\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par debates and implementation. Younger ruling party members have threatened to\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par secede if new leader fails to help pull the nation's economy out of its worst\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par post-war recession.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Whatever the composition of the new cabinet, Japan will still continue to\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par use its traditional method of making decisions by seeking consensus through\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par haggling among political factions and bureaucrats.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Consensus forecasts on key economic indicators for next week (hit ECI/US +\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par F9 for a full list for U.S. data, and ECI/CI + F9 for Canada):\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    WEDNESDAY, JULY 29\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    U.S. June durable goods -0.4 percent after -2.6 percent in May.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     THURSDAY, JULY 30\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    U.S. second quarter employment cost index +0.8 percent after +0.7 percent\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par in Q1\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    FRIDAY, JULY 31\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    U.S. second quarter gross domestic product (GDP) +0.2 percent after +5.4\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par percent in Q1.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Canada's May GDP +0.1 (range: flat to +0.2) after being flat in April.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     (Masaru Sato, Reuters Toronto newsroom (416) 941-8107,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par toronto.newsroom@reuters.com))\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 1998.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du7o0058s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
