\par\par \b \uc2  Canada dollar hits new lows as Greenspan speaks. \b0\par\par\uc2 957 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Juli 1998\par 17:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par FORECAST RANGE   1.4870/1.4930\tab ASIAN CLOSE    1.4878\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par US$ SUPPORT 1.4815  RESISTANCE 1.5035 HIGH 1.4920 LOW 1.4881\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par CANADA OPEN 1.4909 HIGH 1.4926\tab EUROPE OPEN    1.4898\par PREV CLOSE  1.4887 LOW  1.4881\tab HIGH 1.4918 LOW 1.4885\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ------------------------------------------------------------\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par MONDAY, JULY 20\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TREND: BEARISH, ACTIVITY: MODERATE\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par *Canada's dollar continued to weaken vs U.S. dollar, touching a new\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par low of C$1.4926 (US$0.6700) in morning trade. *U.S. dollar\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par gained vs mark and yen after release of Greenspan statement at\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par start of two-day semi-annual Humphrey-Hawkins congressional\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par testimony. *He said higher U.S. inflation would outweigh\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par downside risk arising from Asia's economic downturn. *Canada\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par May retail sales came in as expected, up 0.5 percent after\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par revised 1.1-percent rise in April. *U.S. June housing starts up\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 5.6 percent after revised 1.0-percent fall in May. *U.S. dollar\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par stable ahead of Japan ruling party leader election Friday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TORONTO, July 21 (Reuters) - The Canadian dollar drifted to new lows as the U.S. dollar rebounded against key \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at the start of key testimony by the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  chief.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Canadian unit touched a record trading low of C$1.4926 (US$0.6700) after matching earlier this morning the previous record low of C$1.4920 (US$0.6702) hit overnight in technical trading.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The market is now watching for further comments by U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan at the two-day semi-annual Humphrey-Hawkins congressional testimony kicked off this morning.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "His comment was interpreted as slightly hawkish, the U.S. dollar is above 140 yen... there's no huge surprise there," said Reid Farrill, executive director, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at CIBC Wood Gundy Securities.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Greenspan said higher U.S. inflation would outweigh the downside risk arising from the Asia's economic downturn. His comment triggered selling in North American bonds and the U.S. stock market.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "While we expect that the situation will develop relatively smoothly, the committee believes that, given the current tightness in labour markets, the potential for accelerating inflation is probably greater than the risk of protracted, excessive weakness in the economy," he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In cross trading, the Canadian dollar slipped from earlier levels, but was still up at 1.1975 marks after 1.1967 marks at the previous close here. Canada rose to 93.92 yen from 93.24 yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Last time Greenspan testified, on June 10, North American\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par bond prices surged on his balanced comments and prevailing\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par flight-to-quality capital flows.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     On that day, the Canadian dollar came under speculative\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par selling pressure, which prompted Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par buy Canadian for U.S. dollars.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Back then, Greenspan told the Joint Economic Committee of\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Congress that the Federal Reserve might have to raise interest\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par rates if demand were to continue to exhibit few signs of\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par abating noticeably, but that there was no need for a tighter\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par monetary policy to cool off the economy.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Earlier today after the 0900 EDT/1300 GMT daily window of\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par money market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the Bank of Canada, the Canadian unit\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par had firmed a bit to C$1.4881 (US$0.6720) in a technical move.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "It was sort of stop-loss driven, first (the U.S. dollar)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ran up to the C$1.4905/10 (US$0.6709/0.6707) level. I think\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par there was small nervousness about the bank doing something\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par about the rates, nothing happened, of course," one trader\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "I think people were going to short Canada, long (U.S.)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par dollars, so as a result they didn't like it, a couple of guys\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par sold it (the U.S. dollar) and it fell through," he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Some weakness in the Canadian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which cuts the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par nation's export prices, is needed to offset external shocks,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par such as the slump in Asia and soft commodities prices, said\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Paul Ferley, assistant chief economist, macro economics at Bank\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par of Montreal.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "However, the C$1.4900 (US$0.6711), in our view, that's\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par getting on the weak side and maybe indicating an overshoot," he\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par told Reuters Television.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Canada's latest economic indicator had little impact on the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par market. Canada's May retail sales came in as expected, up 0.5\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par percent after a revised 1.1-percent rise in April. Economists\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par on average had expected a 0.4-percent rise in May.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Separately, U.S. June housing starts rose 5.6 percent after\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par revised a 1.0-percent fall in May.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     "Finally we've got a break from a string of data that were\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par coming in weaker than expected," Ferley said. "I thin the gain\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par is suggesting that consumer spending is still remaining\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par relatively healthy.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par He said he was a bit concerned about the June data in light\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par of slow automobile sales and an expected decline in income as a\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par result of the General Motors strike.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "For the second quarter, we feel there's enough momentum in\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par the economy that we should see growth close to three percent,"\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Ferley said. "If the strike drags on, we may see an impact of\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par maybe up to a percentage point on the third quarter."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The market is also awaiting Friday's election of Japan's\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par new ruling party leader, who will succeed outgoing Prime\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Minister Ryutaro Hashimoto.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The latest speculation among Japanese dailies is that\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Foreign Minister Keizo Obuchi appears to be quietly gaining\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par support within the ruling party against the market's favourite,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par former government spokesman and veteran backroom fixer Seiroku\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Kajiyama, who has proposed drastic banking reform.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     (Masaru Sato, Reuters Toronto newsroom (416) 941-8107,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par toronto.newsroom@reuters.com))\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 1998.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du7l00czg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
