\par\par \b \uc2  Canada dollar ends flat after touching new low. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,156 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Juli 1998\par 22:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par FORECAST RANGE   1.4860/1.4920\tab ASIAN CLOSE    1.4882\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par US$ SUPPORT 1.4815  RESISTANCE 1.5035 HIGH 1.4898 LOW 1.4883\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par CANADA OPEN 1.4884 HIGH 1.4918\tab EUROPE OPEN    1.4880\par PREV CLOSE  1.4890 LOW  1.4870\tab HIGH 1.4894 LOW 1.4878\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ------------------------------------------------------------\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par FRIDAY, JULY 17\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TREND: NEUTRAL, ACTIVITY: MODERATE\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Canada dollar ended flat at C$1.4884 (US$0.6719) after touching a record low of C$1.4918 (US$0.6803) on technical moves in early trade.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par *U.S. May trade deficit jumped to C$15.75 billion, the highest since the current series began in Jan 1992. *Canada's May trade data showed a larger-than-forecast trade surplus of C$1.67 billion after C$1.20 billion in April (exports flat, imports down).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Economists had forecast C$1.22 billion for May.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Yen steady vs U.S. dollar on growing hope that Japan will fix financial problems sooner than\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par later.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Greenspan Humphrey-Hawkins testimony next Tuesday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  <\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TORONTO, July 17 (Reuters) - The Canadian dollar ended flat at C$1.4884 (US$0.6719) on Friday after scratching a record trading low of C$1.4918 (US$0.6703) on technical moves in early trade.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  slipped to an 18-month low against the mark as the German \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  firmed versus other \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on brighter prospects for financial reform in Russia, backed by international aid.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Canadian unit has been sliding against the U.S. dollar constantly for the past four months from the C$1.4070/80 level (US$0.7107/0.71020), and still lacks support on the chart. Canada has weakened by 100 points this week, with the U.S. dollar firming by a full Canadian cent.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "As we go forward, I think people will sell the Canadian dollar initially and just to see if there's any continued follow-through in the weakness in Canada," one trader said. "Right now I don't think the market is terribly in love with the Canadian dollar, they prefer to short (sell) it."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On the downside of the U.S. dollar, short-term traders would buy back Canadian dollars at C$1.4845/50 (US$0.6736/0.6734), he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Trading is expected to remain cautious as U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan, the world's most influential central banker, discusses the U.S. economy in his Humphrey-Hawkins congressional testimony next Tuesday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The move this morning to touch the lowest point was largely anticipated as traders held their breath as there was the remote possibility Canada's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would raise a key lending rate at the 0900 EDT/1300 GMT window for its money market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and found that there was no support from the money side.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Little direct impact was felt from Canada's May trade data, which showed a bigger-than-expected C$1.67 billion in trade surplus after C$1.20 billion in April. Economists had forecast C$1.22 billion for May.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The trade report confirms that domestic demand may have downshifted somewhat in recent months, suggesting less reason for the Bank of Canada to raise interest rates to support the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," said Sal Guatieri, senior economist at Bank of Montreal.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Despite the rise in trade surplus, Canada's current account deficit is not shrinking, which means the nation still has to draw overseas funds to finance its debt.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "This is supportive of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , there is no question there," Randall Powley, senior economist at Scotia Capital Markets, told Reuters Television. "It's a supportive move, although most of the weakness in the Canadian dollar hasn't been domestically driven... so we can't say this is going to be lasting support for the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A recent spate of weak domestic economic data have weighed on the battered Canadian dollar. But it has been under downward pressure from generally weak commodities prices, which was brought on by slack Asian demand. Capital has also been flowing into U.S. dollar assets as they maintain safe-haven status.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In cross trading, the Canadian dollar extended losses to 1.1960 marks, the lowest level since mid-January 1997, from 1.1994 marks on Thursday, which was the weakest since April 1997. Canada fell to 93.66 yen from 94.03 yen at the previous close here.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The yen was steady against the U.S. dollar on growing hopes that Japan will fix its economic and banking problems sooner than later as candidates for the next premiership disclose plans to fight the recession and reform the financial system, one factor hampering the path to a quick turnaround in business and consumer sentiment.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    In recent trade, the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has staged \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  occasionally  to smooth out fluctuations, but has held off\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par raising interest rates to defend the value of the local dollar.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     "I think they have an appropriate policy. The economy is in\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par reasonable shape, although growth numbers are more mixed\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par lately. That suggests they shouldn't be in a tightening\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par position. There's certainly no inflation pressure on the bank,"\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Powley said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Canada's consumer price index (CPI) showed tame inflation\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par on Thursday, with overall CPI rising 1.0 percent year on year\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par in June after a 1.1-percent rise in May, and the core rate\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par rising 1.0 percent after a 1.4 percent rise, both below\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par expectations.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has indicated that wider fluctuations of\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par monetary conditions are expected in Canada. It is watching\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par movements in the nation's short-term interest rates and the\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's performance against other Group of 10 \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par any signs of drastic easing in overall money conditions.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was last seen \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par to slow the pace of depreciation on Wednesday, when the decline\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par was precipitous.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     The latest recent selling came from a string of weak\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par economic domestic economic data.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     On Wednesday, May manufacturing shipments showed a fall of\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1.0 percent after a revised drop of 0.7 percent in April, while\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par economists had forecast a 0.6-percent rise. May car sales came\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par in lower than expected, rising 1.1 percent after a 5.9-percent\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par rise in April.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Last week, the Canadian unit came under heavy selling after\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par the nation's employment fell for the second consecutive month\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par in June, by 35,900 to 14.24 million after a drop of 7,300 in\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par May. Housing starts dropped 4.6 percent in June, the third straight month of\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par decline.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Consensus forecasts for key economic indicators for the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par week of July 20:\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  <\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Monday, July 20\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Canada May wholesale trade +0.5 percent (+0.1 percent in April)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Tuesday, July 21\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Canada May retail trade +0.4 percent (+1.0 percent in April)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (Masaru Sato, Reuters Toronto newsroom (416) 941-8107,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par toronto.newsroom@reuters.com))\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 1998.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du7h00p1x\par }\page 