\par\par \b \uc2  Canada bonds open weaker in line with U.S. market. \b0\par\par\uc2 737 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Juli 1998\par 15:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TORONTO, July 16 (Reuters) - Canadian bonds opened weaker in stable trading on Thursday, following U.S. treasuries. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar's slide below 140 yen on hopes of stronger Japanese leadership took some shine off the safe-haven status of U.S. dollar assets, which spilled over into Canadian bonds. \par \par \uc2  The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  extended overnight gains on growing expectations in the market that former chief cabinet secretary Seiroku Kajiyama will win the race for the leader of Japan's ruling Liberal Democratic Party (LDP) to succeed outgoing Prime Minister Ryutaro Hashimoto. \par \par \uc2  The market favours drastic banking reform plans Kajiyama has been proposing since he quit his cabinet post in the previous Hashimoto government. \par \par \uc2  Foreign Minister Keizo Obuchi, seen as a safe choice but lacking strong leadership, is still a front runner in the LDP election, which has been delayed by three days till July 24. \par \par \uc2  Canada's benchmark 30-year bond fell C$0.16 to C$135.30, yielding 5.536 percent. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. 30-year bond fell 8/32, yielding 5.72 percent. The U.S.-Canada spread was 18 basis points, unchanged from the previous close here. \par \par \uc2  The market failed to react favourably to a decline in U.S. industrial output data that suggests the pace of U.S. growth is slowing and poses little inflationary threat to bond holders. \par \par \uc2  June industrial output fell 0.6 percent after a 0.3-percent rise in May, mainly hit by General Motors strikes. Capacity utilization slipped to 81.6 from 82.4. \par \par \uc2  Earlier in the morning, Canada's June consumer price index (CPI) showed tame inflation, but had little direct impact on the market. \par \par \uc2  Overall CPI rose 1.0 percent year on year in June after a 1.1-percent rise in May, and the core rate (ex-food and energy prices) rose 1.0 percent from a year earlier after a 1.4 percent rise. \par \par \uc2  Economists on average had forecast a 1.1-percent rise for all items and an increase of 1.3 percent for the core rate. \par \par \uc2  "The year-over-year increase of 1.0 percent moves back down to the floor of the (\b \highlight22\uc2 central) bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 1-3 percent target band, so there's no reason on the basis of inflation data why they should be raising rates," said Rob Palombi, senior fixed-income analyst at Standard & Poor's MMS. \par \par \uc2  "In my view, the Bank of Canada is going to have to raise rates, but I don't think that's yet a majority. I think they are going to have to go regardless of the CPI numbers, said Jim Webber, director of fixed-income research at TD Securities Inc. \par \par \uc2  He pointed to the underperformance in Canadian bonds and bills against the U.S. market on Wednesday, which means that the contagion of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is spreading to other assets. \par \par \uc2  The Canadian unit, which has been sliding constantly for the past four months, hit a record trading low of C$1.4895 (US$0.6714) on Wednesday with \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  slowing the pace of steep depreciation. \par \par \uc2  The most recent selling came from a string of weak economic domestic economic data. \par \par \uc2  Consensus forecasts for key Canadian economic indicators for the rest of the week (hit ECI/CI + F9 for a full list): \par \par \uc2  Friday, JULY 17 \par \par \uc2  May trade surplus C$1.22 billion (April: C$1.20 billion). \par \par \uc2  The money market was stable this morning after weakening sharply on the falling Canadian dollar on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "We've weakened off and that's brought buyers back into the market," said one trader. "We personally did not believe that the bank will raise rates today. I think we're a couple of weeks away or we need to have a very very big move in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  like when we come in tomorrow morning, and the dollar is at C$1.5000 (US$0.6667), that's a problem and the (\b \highlight22\uc2 central) bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will go." \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Canada's signal to the market so far has been "business as usual" and its tone hasn't changed, she said. \par \par \uc2  Canada's three-month when issued T-bill traded with a yield of 4.95 percent, unchanged from the previous close here. \par \par \uc2  ((Masaru Sato, Reuters Toronto newsroom (416) 941-8107, toronto.newsroom@reuters.com)) \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du7g00m94\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
