\par\par \b \uc2  Canada Bonds Down On  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Plunge, Rate Hike Fears \b0\par\par\uc2 401 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Juli 1998\par 22:23\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    TORONTO (Dow Jones)--Canadian bonds ended down in fairly\par active trading Wednesday, with the sell-off sparked by the\par plummeting Canadian dollar and a revival of fears that the\par Bank of Canada may soon be forced to raise interest rates to\par defend the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par    5.00s 2000 99.65   dn 0.10  5.22% vs 5.16%\par    5.50s 2002 100.65  dn 0.20  5.31% vs 5.27%\par    6.00s 2008 104.76  dn 0.40  5.37% vs 5.33%\par    8.00s 2027 135.41  dn 0.50  5.53% vs 5.50%\par    10-Yr Spread To U.S. 10-yr: -0.10 vs -0.14\par    CGB SEP 98 124.91  dn 0.46 volume 11,529\par    Analysts noted that while Canadian bonds usually look to\par the U.S. Treasury market for direction, today's mildly\par positive tone for U.S. bonds could do nothing to dispel the\par gloom cast by Canada's weakening \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which established\par new all-time lows against the U.S. dollar for the fourth\par straight day on Wednesday.\par    After opening near a U.S.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  dollar level of C$1.4823, the\par Canadian dollar drifted steadily lower, prompting the Bank of\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in its defense several times in the\par afternoon session after the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  topped the C$1.4870\par mark. The U.S. unit eventually rose to above the C$1.4890\par level late Wednesday.\par    The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's dive set the tone for Canadian bonds as\par well, analysts said.\par    "There's been spillover from one market to the other,\par obviously," said Avery Shenfeld, senior economist at CIBC\par Wood Gundy Securities Inc., who noted that the Canadian\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's descent into uncharted lows over the past several\par days has heightened market jitters about the possibility that\par Canada's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may soon lead interest rates higher.\par    "There's fear starting to build now that we're getting\par closer to a rate hike," he said. "But we're still not there\par yet, and that's why the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is still moving down."\par    Some said the market also took note in the early morning\par of disappointing Canadian May manufacturing data, which\par suggests that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and bond market weakness may be\par further exacerbated if Friday's release of May merchandise\par trade statistics are also undistinguished.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires  07-15-98\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1623EDT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1182 4152 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020010916du7f01pq4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
