\par\par \b \uc2  Canadian dollar ends weak, hits new low. \b0\par\par\uc2 983 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Juli 1998\par 22:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par FORECAST RANGE   1.4740/1.4850\tab ASIAN CLOSE    1.4757\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par US$ SUPPORT 1.4680  RESISTANCE 1.4900 HIGH 1.4790 LOW 1.4744\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par CANADA OPEN 1.4788 HIGH 1.4800\tab EUROPE OPEN    1.4754\par PREV CLOSE  1.4747 LOW  1.4744\tab HIGH 1.4788 LOW 1.4746\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ------------------------------------------------------------\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par FRIDAY, JULY 10\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TREND: BEARISH ACTIVITY: MODERATE\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par *Canada dollar ended at record closing low of C$1.4782\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (US$0.6765) after touching key psychological point and record\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par intra-day low of C$1.4800 (US$0.6757) in afternoon trade.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par *Selling emerged in early trade as Canada's jobs data came in\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par weaker than expected. *Bank of Canada seen absent from market\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par as \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  slide was gradual. *Employment fell for second\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par consecutive month in June, by 35,900 with most drops in farm\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par sector, after drop of 7,300 in May. *Economists on average had\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par forecast employment rising 26,000. *Full impact of GM strike\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par seen coming in July data. *Jobless rate unchanged at 8.4 pct in\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par June.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TORONTO, July 10 (Reuters) - Canada's dollar slumped on Friday to a record closing low of C$1.4783 (US$0.6765), after touching a new intra-day low of C$1.4800 (US$0.6757) in gradual selling triggered by the nation's weak jobs data.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par There was no indication of market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  or an exchange rate check by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to stem a further decline in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which has been fairly orderly.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "We tested the C$1.48 big figure level, but did not breach it. The market is certainly poised to move through that price point next week," one risk manager said. "The basic mood is still bearish and the Bank of Canada has been noticeably quiet, or totally absent from the market not only in terms of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  but in terms of commenting."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "There's not a real sense of panic yet in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  being sold off," another trader said, adding that market players were leaving for home with either square or small U.S. dollar long (buy) positions in spot, short-term trading.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "I think some people may be looking out to put on long Canada positions on a longer-term basis, maybe constructing their positions for option plays," he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Economists and market participants expect Canada's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to hold off raising its key lending rate for a while. Such a move would would be expected to lure investors into Canadian assets, boosting dollar demand.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  took another hit in typically thin summer trading in North America as traders, who had expected some growth in job creation, renewed Canadian dollar selling on early morning news that employment in Canada fell for the second consecutive month in June.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Total employment fell by 35,900 to 14.24 million with most declines recorded in the agricultural sector, after a drop of 7,300 in May. The jobless rate was unchanged at 8.4 percent.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Canadians may see more weakness on the jobs front in July as the impact of the General Motors plant shutdowns in North America was not fully reflected in the latest data.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The Canadian dollar story has been a global story until today. The weakness in the exchange rate was clearly reflecting the weakness in the commodities prices, which are relevant to Canada," said Andrew Spence, senior economist at Deutsche Bank Securities. "However, we're beginning to see signs of slowdown domestically, and the question is if that slowdown is temporary or something more permanent."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Canada's bearish jobs report came on the heels of Thursday's news of a 4.6 percent drop in June housing starts, making the third consecutive month of declines. Construction industry strikes in the key province of Ontario were blamed.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In cross trading, Canada fell to 1.2283 marks from 1.2390 marks at the previous close here, and to 95.44 yen from 95.53 yen, moving in tight ranges in response to the U.S. dollar's fluctuations against the mark and yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The U.S. dollar fell to mid-1.81 mark levels as the market turned optimistic from Thursday's more pessimistic views on Russia's tackling economic challenges and its talks with the International Monetary Fund for receiving loans. This pulled the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  down to 140 yen before it rebounded to around 141 yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Financial markets remained wary because of the uncertainty over whether\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Japan's ruling party under Prime Minister Ryutaro Hashimoto would survive the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Upper House election on Sunday and deliver on promised fiscal stimulus\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par measures.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     The Canadian dollar is vulnerable to safe-haven capital flows to the United\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par States. The U.S. dollar has in the past firmed against all major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par times when there was concern over the world's troubled regions, such as Asia\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par and Russia.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     The market was a bit jittery ahead of the 0900 EDT/1300 GMT window for Bank\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par of Canada money market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  this morning, although most traders believed\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would hold back from raising its key lending rate.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  published its weekly monetary conditions index (MCI) data,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par which showed the nation's overall money conditions loosening to -5.83 from\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -5.78 a week ago.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Consensus forecasts for key Canadian economic indicators for next week (hit\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ECI/CI + F9 for a full list):\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Wednesday, JULY 15\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    May manufacturing shipments +0.6 percent (April: -0.6)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    May new motor vehicle sales +1.6 percent (April: +5.9)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Thursday, JULY 16\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    June CPI all items +1.1 percent (May: +1.1)\par 